{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10478","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10478","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10478","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oluwaseyi","familyName":"Giwa","name":"Giwa, Oluwaseyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Adewole","name":"Adewole, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tobi","familyName":"Awodumila","name":"Awodumila, Tobi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pelumi","familyName":"Aderinto","name":"Aderinto, Pelumi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The LLM as a Network Operator: A Vision for Generative AI in the 6G Radio Access Network"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-27T20:47:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:00:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The management of future AI-native Next-Generation (NextG) Radio Access Networks (RANs), including 6G and beyond, presents a challenge of immense complexity that exceeds the capabilities of traditional automation. In response, we introduce the concept of the LLM-RAN Operator. In this paradigm, a Large Language Model (LLM) is embedded into the RAN control loop to translate high-level human intents into optimal network actions. Unlike prior empirical studies, we present a formal framework for an LLM-RAN operator that builds on earlier work by making guarantees checkable through an adapter aligned with the Open RAN (O-RAN) standard, separating strategic LLM-driven guidance in the Non-Real-Time (RT) RAN intelligent controller (RIC) from reactive execution in the Near-RT RIC, including a proposition on policy expressiveness and a theorem on convergence to stable fixed points. By framing the problem with mathematical rigor, our work provides the analytical tools to reason about the feasibility and stability of AI-native RAN control. It identifies critical research challenges in safety, real-time performance, and physical-world grounding. This paper aims to bridge the gap between AI theory and wireless systems engineering in the NextG era, aligning with the AI4NextG vision to develop knowledgeable, intent-driven wireless networks that integrate generative AI into the heart of the RAN."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to Workshop on AI and ML for Next-Generation Wireless Communications and Networking, NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10478","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:40:12Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:40:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:40:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10518","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10518","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10518","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Justin","familyName":"Arndt","name":"Arndt, Justin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Holographic Knowledge Manifolds: A Novel Pipeline for Continual Learning Without Catastrophic Forgetting in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-03T16:54:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:01:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-21T00:59:21Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v2; This paper includes significant errors discovered post publication by the author"},{"date":"2025-11-24T01:11:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce the Holographic Knowledge Manifold (HKM), a four-phase pipeline that achieves zero catastrophic forgetting in AI knowledge representation while maintaining minimal memory growth and high efficiency. Leveraging fractal quantization, probabilistic entanglement, and dynamic diffraction chipping, HKM compresses knowledge substrates by 3x with 67% storage savings, integrates holographically at 100%, and supports over 1,020 updates with 1% growth per increment. In experiments on combined WikiText and FB15k datasets (scaled to 2,997 nodes), we demonstrate industry-leading performance: 0% forgetting (infinite improvement over GEM baselines), 3x compression, and 53% training time reduction on consumer GPU hardware. Hypothetical cost analyses project $92.4M savings over 5 years at petabyte scale, with 21.2% energy reduction and 33% lower carbon footprint. This work hypothesizes a paradigm shift for public large language models (LLMs), enabling \"eternal\" adaptation without retraining. Future extensions to multimodal fusion and quantum hardware could further democratize scalable AI, potentially reducing fine-tuning costs by 60-80% for models like Llama-3 or Grok-4. Code, datasets, and full results are publicly available for reproducibility."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper includes significant errors discovered post publication by the author"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10518","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:41:09Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:41:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-24T02:48:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10519","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10519","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10519","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Chang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wayne","familyName":"Burleson","name":"Burleson, Wayne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giovanni","familyName":"De Micheli","name":"De Micheli, Giovanni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-03T16:57:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:01:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Approximate multipliers (AppMults) are widely used in deep learning accelerators to reduce their area, delay, and power consumption. However, AppMults introduce arithmetic errors into deep learning models, necessitating a retraining process to recover accuracy. A key step in retraining is computing the gradient of the AppMult, i.e., the partial derivative of the approximate product with respect to each input operand. Existing approaches typically estimate this gradient using that of the accurate multiplier (AccMult), which can lead to suboptimal retraining results. To address this, we propose two methods to obtain more precise gradients of AppMults. The first, called LUT-2D, characterizes the AppMult gradient with 2-dimensional lookup tables (LUTs), providing fine-grained estimation and achieving the highest retraining accuracy. The second, called LUT-1D, is a compact and more efficient variant that stores gradient values in 1-dimensional LUTs, achieving comparable retraining accuracy with shorter runtime. Experimental results show that on CIFAR-10 with convolutional neural networks, our LUT-2D and LUT-1D methods improve retraining accuracy by 3.83% and 3.72% on average, respectively. On ImageNet with vision transformer models, our LUT-1D method improves retraining accuracy by 23.69% on average, compared to a state-of-the-art retraining framework."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10519","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:41:10Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:41:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:41:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10530","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10530","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10530","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiexiong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiexiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"ji","name":"ji, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dynamic Adaptive Shared Experts with Grouped Multi-Head Attention Mixture of Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-05T02:49:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:01:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformer models based on the Mixture of Experts (MoE) architecture have made significant progress in long-sequence modeling, but existing models still have shortcomings in computational efficiency and the ability to capture long-range dependencies, especially in terms of the dynamic adaptability of expert resource allocation. In this paper, we propose a Dynamic Adaptive Shared Expert and Grouped Multi-Head Attention Hybrid Model (DASG-MoE) to enhance long-sequence modeling capabilities by integrating three modules. First, we employ the Grouped Multi-Head Attention (GMHA) mechanism to effectively reduce the computational complexity of long sequences. By parallel processing through sequence grouping, local sliding window attention, and feature aggregation, we address long-range dependency issues and the model's lack of generalization for local information. Second, we design a Dual-Scale Shared Expert Structure (DSSE), where shallow experts use lightweight computations to quickly respond to low-dimensional features, while deep experts process high-dimensional complex semantics through pre-training transfer and post-training optimization, achieving a dynamic balance between efficiency and accuracy. Third, we propose a hierarchical Adaptive Dynamic Routing (ADR) mechanism that dynamically selects expert levels based on feature complexity and task requirements, and optimizes resource allocation through a local expert activation strategy. Experiments on multiple long-sequence benchmark datasets demonstrate that our DASG-MoE model outperforms state-of-the-art models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10530","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:41:25Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:41:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:41:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10570","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10570","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10570","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengen","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Shengen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sisuo","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Sisuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haicheng","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Haicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Limin","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Limin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiping","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Weiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runwei","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Runwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutao","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Yutao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Large Foundation Models for Trajectory Prediction in Autonomous Driving: A Comprehensive Survey"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T10:30:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:02:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Trajectory prediction serves as a critical functionality in autonomous driving, enabling the anticipation of future motion paths for traffic participants such as vehicles and pedestrians, which is essential for driving safety. Although conventional deep learning methods have improved accuracy, they remain hindered by inherent limitations, including lack of interpretability, heavy reliance on large-scale annotated data, and weak generalization in long-tail scenarios. The rise of Large Foundation Models (LFMs) is transforming the research paradigm of trajectory prediction. This survey offers a systematic review of recent advances in LFMs, particularly Large Language Models (LLMs) and Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) for trajectory prediction. By integrating linguistic and scene semantics, LFMs facilitate interpretable contextual reasoning, significantly enhancing prediction safety and generalization in complex environments. The article highlights three core methodologies: trajectory-language mapping, multimodal fusion, and constraint-based reasoning. It covers prediction tasks for both vehicles and pedestrians, evaluation metrics, and dataset analyses. Key challenges such as computational latency, data scarcity, and real-world robustness are discussed, along with future research directions including low-latency inference, causality-aware modeling, and motion foundation models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10570","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:42:22Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:42:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:42:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10584","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10584","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10584","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xiaofan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zisu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zisu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Janice","familyName":"Krieger","name":"Krieger, Janice","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohan","familyName":"Zalake","name":"Zalake, Mohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Smart Trial: Evaluating the Use of Large Language Models for Recruiting Clinical Trial Participants via Social Media"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T01:34:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:03:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Clinical trials (CT) are essential for advancing medical research and treatment, yet efficiently recruiting eligible participants -- each of whom must meet complex eligibility criteria -- remains a significant challenge. Traditional recruitment approaches, such as advertisements or electronic health record screening within hospitals, are often time-consuming and geographically constrained. This work addresses the recruitment challenge by leveraging the vast amount of health-related information individuals share on social media platforms. With the emergence of powerful large language models (LLMs) capable of sophisticated text understanding, we pose the central research question: Can LLM-driven tools facilitate CT recruitment by identifying potential participants through their engagement on social media? To investigate this question, we introduce TRIALQA, a novel dataset comprising two social media collections from the subreddits on colon cancer and prostate cancer. Using eligibility criteria from public real-world CTs, experienced annotators are hired to annotate TRIALQA to indicate (1) whether a social media user meets a given eligibility criterion and (2) the user's stated reasons for interest in participating in CT. We benchmark seven widely used LLMs on these two prediction tasks, employing six distinct training and inference strategies. Our extensive experiments reveal that, while LLMs show considerable promise, they still face challenges in performing the complex, multi-hop reasoning needed to accurately assess eligibility criteria."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10584","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:42:41Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:42:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:42:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10641","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10641","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10641","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikita","familyName":"Rajaneesh","name":"Rajaneesh, Nikita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Zollo","name":"Zollo, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Zemel","name":"Zemel, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Test-Time Warmup for Multimodal Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T18:58:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:05:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-06T12:24:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-07T01:39:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) hold great promise for advanced reasoning at the intersection of text and images, yet they have not fully realized this potential. MLLMs typically integrate an LLM, a vision encoder, and a connector that maps the vision encoder's embeddings into the LLM's text embedding space. Although each component is pretrained on massive datasets with billions of samples, the entire multimodal model is typically trained on only thousands (or a few million) samples, which can result in weak performance on complex reasoning tasks. To address these shortcomings, instead of relying on extensive labeled datasets for fine-tuning, we propose a Test-Time Warmup method that adapts the MLLM per test instance by leveraging data from weakly supervised auxiliary tasks. With our approach, we observe a relative performance improvement of 4.03% on MMMU, 5.28% on VQA-Rad, and 1.63% on GQA on the Llama-Vision-Instruct model. Our method demonstrates that 'warming up' before inference can enhance MLLMs' robustness across diverse reasoning tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10641","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:44:00Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:44:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-07T03:05:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10659","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10659","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10659","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor M.","familyName":"Castillo","name":"Castillo, Victor M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeping","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yeping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"M4GN: Mesh-based Multi-segment Hierarchical Graph Network for Dynamic Simulations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T19:38:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:06:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mesh-based graph neural networks (GNNs) have become effective surrogates for PDE simulations, yet their deep message passing incurs high cost and over-smoothing on large, long-range meshes; hierarchical GNNs shorten propagation paths but still face two key obstacles: (i) building coarse graphs that respect mesh topology, geometry, and physical discontinuities, and (ii) maintaining fine-scale accuracy without sacrificing the speed gained from coarsening. We tackle these challenges with M4GN, a three-tier, segment-centric hierarchical network. M4GN begins with a hybrid segmentation strategy that pairs a fast graph partitioner with a superpixel-style refinement guided by modal-decomposition features, producing contiguous segments of dynamically consistent nodes. These segments are encoded by a permutation-invariant aggregator, avoiding the order sensitivity and quadratic cost of aggregation approaches used in prior works. The resulting information bridges a micro-level GNN, which captures local dynamics, and a macro-level transformer that reasons efficiently across segments, achieving a principled balance between accuracy and efficiency. Evaluated on multiple representative benchmark datasets, M4GN improves prediction accuracy by up to 56% while achieving up to 22% faster inference than state-of-the-art baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted and published in Transactions on Machine Learning Research (TMLR), 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10659","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:44:26Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:44:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:44:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10691","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10691","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10691","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fardin Jalil","familyName":"Piran","name":"Piran, Fardin Jalil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiling","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhiling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianyu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Qianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiong","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farhad","familyName":"Imani","name":"Imani, Farhad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Privacy-Preserving Decentralized Federated Learning via Explainable Adaptive Differential Privacy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T20:52:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:09:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-06T18:31:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:35:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-16T06:49:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-18T00:48:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Decentralized federated learning enables collaborative model training without a central server, but shared model updates can still leak sensitive information through inversion, reconstruction, and membership inference attacks. Differential privacy offers formal protection, yet existing decentralized methods operate without visibility into the noise already injected by previous participants. Each client therefore adds a full, worst-case perturbation at every step, and the accumulated noise degrades accuracy well below what the privacy requirement actually demands. We present PrivateDFL, a decentralized and privacy-preserving framework that pairs hyperdimensional computing with a transparent noise accountant. The accountant tracks the cumulative perturbation present in the shared model and lets each client add only the minimal incremental noise needed to satisfy its privacy budget. We prove that every transmitted model satisfies the target privacy guarantee, and that under this accounting the cumulative noise grows only logarithmically in the number of clients and rounds, rather than the far faster super-linear growth incurred without accounting. This yields a substantially tighter balance between privacy and accuracy than prior approaches. Across image, speech, and wearable-sensor benchmarks, and under both identically and non-identically distributed data, PrivateDFL surpasses centrally trained Transformer-based and deep neural network baselines, improving accuracy by 16 percent on images, 62 percent on speech, and 14 percent on wearable sensing over the strongest baseline in each case, while reducing inference latency by up to 119 times and energy consumption by up to 143 times. These properties make PrivateDFL a practical solution for privacy-preserving collaborative learning in settings where sensitive data cannot be centralized, such as healthcare and human-activity monitoring."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10691","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:10Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T03:56:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10695","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10695","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10695","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoming","familyName":"Jing","name":"Jing, Haoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oren","familyName":"Wright","name":"Wright, Oren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"José M. F.","familyName":"Moura","name":"Moura, José M. F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yorie","familyName":"Nakahira","name":"Nakahira, Yorie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Kalman Bayesian Transformer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T21:15:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:09:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sequential fine-tuning of transformers is useful when new data arrive sequentially, especially with shifting distributions. Unlike batch learning, sequential learning demands that training be stabilized despite a small amount of data by balancing new information and previously learned knowledge in the pre-trained models. This challenge is further complicated when training is to be completed in latency-critical environments and learning must additionally quantify and be mediated by uncertainty. Motivated by these challenges, we propose a novel method that frames sequential fine-tuning as a posterior inference problem within a Bayesian framework. Our approach integrates closed-form moment propagation of random variables, Kalman Bayesian Neural Networks, and Taylor approximations of the moments of softmax functions. By explicitly accounting for pre-trained models as priors and adaptively balancing them against new information based on quantified uncertainty, our method achieves robust and data-efficient sequential learning. The effectiveness of our method is demonstrated through numerical simulations involving sequential adaptation of a decision transformer to tasks characterized by distribution shifts and limited memory resources."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the 64th IEEE Conference on Decision and Control (CDC 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10695","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:16Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:45:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10739","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10739","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10739","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mobina","familyName":"Pournemat","name":"Pournemat, Mobina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keivan","familyName":"Rezaei","name":"Rezaei, Keivan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaurang","familyName":"Sriramanan","name":"Sriramanan, Gaurang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arman","familyName":"Zarei","name":"Zarei, Arman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxiang","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Jiaxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid","familyName":"Eghbalzadeh","name":"Eghbalzadeh, Hamid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soheil","familyName":"Feizi","name":"Feizi, Soheil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning Under Uncertainty: Exploring Probabilistic Reasoning Capabilities of LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T22:58:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:11:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-26T15:28:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-29T01:10:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite widespread success in language understanding and generation, large language models (LLMs) exhibit unclear and often inconsistent behavior when faced with tasks that require probabilistic reasoning. In this work, we present the first comprehensive study of the reasoning capabilities of LLMs over explicit discrete probability distributions. Given observations from a probability distribution, we evaluate models on three carefully designed tasks, mode identification, maximum likelihood estimation, and sample generation, by prompting them to provide responses to queries about either the joint distribution or its conditionals. These tasks thus probe a range of probabilistic skills, including frequency analysis, marginalization, and generative behavior. Through comprehensive empirical evaluations, we demonstrate that there exists a clear performance gap between smaller and larger models, with the latter demonstrating stronger inference and surprising capabilities in sample generation. Furthermore, our investigations reveal notable limitations, including sensitivity to variations in the notation utilized to represent probabilistic outcomes and performance degradation of over 60% as context length increases. Together, our results provide a detailed understanding of the probabilistic reasoning abilities of LLMs and identify key directions for future improvement."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10739","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:46:18Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:46:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-29T02:56:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10746","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10746","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10746","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adarsh","familyName":"Srinivasan","name":"Srinivasan, Adarsh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob","familyName":"Dineen","name":"Dineen, Jacob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Umar","familyName":"Afzal","name":"Afzal, Muhammad Umar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Uzair","familyName":"Sarfraz","name":"Sarfraz, Muhammad Uzair","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irbaz B.","familyName":"Riaz","name":"Riaz, Irbaz B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RECAP: Transparent Inference-Time Emotion Alignment for Medical Dialogue Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T23:30:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:12:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-03T02:09:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-04T01:16:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-03T17:31:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-05T01:08:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models in healthcare often produce emotionally flat or opaque responses, failing to provide the transparent reasoning required for clinical trust. We present RECAP (Reflect-Extract-Calibrate-Align-Produce), an inference-time framework grounded in cognitive appraisal theory that decomposes patient input into auditable, appraisal-theoretic stages without retraining. Across multiple benchmarks and models from 8B to 120B parameters, RECAP improves alignment with human judgments, with gains inversely proportional to model scale. Intermediate outputs further reveal that models systematically underweight relational factors such as social support. In blinded evaluations, oncology fellows rated RECAP responses significantly higher than baselines with 76-88% win rates, demonstrating that principled prompting can enhance medical AI's emotional intelligence while maintaining the transparency required for clinical deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"LREC 2026: ClinicalNLP Workshop (Oral)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10746","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:46:28Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:46:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-05T04:00:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10761","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10761","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10761","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcelo","familyName":"Sandoval-Castaneda","name":"Sandoval-Castaneda, Marcelo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bryan","familyName":"Russell","name":"Russell, Bryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josef","familyName":"Sivic","name":"Sivic, Josef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gregory","familyName":"Shakhnarovich","name":"Shakhnarovich, Gregory","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian Caba","familyName":"Heilbron","name":"Heilbron, Fabian Caba","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EditDuet: A Multi-Agent System for Video Non-Linear Editing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T00:27:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-30T00:26:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3721238.3730761","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated tools for video editing and assembly have applications ranging from filmmaking and advertisement to content creation for social media. Previous video editing work has mainly focused on either retrieval or user interfaces, leaving actual editing to the user. In contrast, we propose to automate the core task of video editing, formulating it as sequential decision making process. Ours is a multi-agent approach. We design an Editor agent and a Critic agent. The Editor takes as input a collection of video clips together with natural language instructions and uses tools commonly found in video editing software to produce an edited sequence. On the other hand, the Critic gives natural language feedback to the editor based on the produced sequence or renders it if it is satisfactory. We introduce a learning-based approach for enabling effective communication across specialized agents to address the language-driven video editing task. Finally, we explore an LLM-as-a-judge metric for evaluating the quality of video editing system and compare it with general human preference. We evaluate our system's output video sequences qualitatively and quantitatively through a user study and find that our system vastly outperforms existing approaches in terms of coverage, time constraint satisfaction, and human preference."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"SIGGRAPH 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10761","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-16T02:46:49Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:46:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-30T02:19:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10809","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10809","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10809","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio","familyName":"Bărbălau","name":"Bărbălau, Antonio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cristian Daniel","familyName":"Păduraru","name":"Păduraru, Cristian Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teodor","familyName":"Poncu","name":"Poncu, Teodor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandru","familyName":"Tifrea","name":"Tifrea, Alexandru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elena","familyName":"Burceanu","name":"Burceanu, Elena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rethinking Sparse Autoencoders: Select-and-Project for Fairness and Control from Encoder Features Alone"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T06:36:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:17:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-05T13:23:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-08T01:36:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs) are widely employed for mechanistic interpretability and model steering. Within this context, steering is by design performed by means of decoding altered SAE intermediate representations. This procedure essentially rewrites the original activations as a weighted sum of decoder features. In contrast to existing literature, we forward an encoder-centric alternative to model steering which demonstrates a stronger cross-modal performance. We introduce S\u0026amp;P Top-K, a retraining-free and computationally lightweight Selection and Projection framework that identifies Top-K encoder features aligned with a sensitive attribute or behavior, optionally aggregates them into a single control axis, and computes an orthogonal projection to be subsequently applied directly in the model's native embedding space. In vision-language models, it improves fairness metrics on CelebA and FairFace by up to 3.2 times over conventional SAE usage, and in large language models, it substantially reduces aggressiveness and sycophancy in Llama-3 8B Instruct, achieving up to 3.6 times gains over masked reconstruction. These findings suggest that encoder-centric interventions provide a general, efficient, and more effective mechanism for shaping model behavior at inference time than the traditional decoder-centric use of SAEs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10809","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:47:56Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:47:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-08T02:47:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10918","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10918","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10918","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbo","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Wenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoyi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Shaoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ToMA: Token Merge with Attention for Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T17:35:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:23:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T02:10:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:22:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-30T17:46:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:53:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models excel in high-fidelity image generation but face scalability limits due to transformers' quadratic attention complexity. Plug-and-play token reduction methods like ToMeSD and ToFu reduce FLOPs by merging redundant tokens in generated images but rely on GPU-inefficient operations (e.g., sorting, scattered writes), introducing overheads that negate theoretical speedups when paired with optimized attention implementations (e.g., FlashAttention). To bridge this gap, we propose Token Merge with Attention (ToMA), an off-the-shelf method that redesigns token reduction for GPU-aligned efficiency, with three key contributions: 1) a reformulation of token merge as a submodular optimization problem to select diverse tokens; 2) merge/unmerge as an attention-like linear transformation via GPU-friendly matrix operations; and 3) exploiting latent locality and sequential redundancy (pattern reuse) to minimize overhead. ToMA reduces SDXL/Flux generation latency by 24%/23%, respectively (with DINO $Δ\u0026lt; 0.07$), outperforming prior methods. This work bridges the gap between theoretical and practical efficiency for transformers in diffusion. Code available at https://github.com/WenboLuu/ToMA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"In proceedings of the 42nd International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2025). Code available at https://github.com/wenboluu/ToMA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10918","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:50:31Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:50:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-02T04:46:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10932","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10932","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10932","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seongho","familyName":"Joo","name":"Joo, Seongho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyukhun","familyName":"Koh","name":"Koh, Hyukhun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyomin","familyName":"Jung","name":"Jung, Kyomin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Public Data Assisted Differentially Private In-Context Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T18:11:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:24:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In-context learning (ICL) in Large Language Models (LLMs) has shown remarkable performance across various tasks without requiring fine-tuning. However, recent studies have highlighted the risk of private data leakage through the prompt in ICL, especially when LLMs are exposed to malicious attacks. While differential privacy (DP) provides strong privacy guarantees, it often significantly reduces the utility of in-context learning (ICL). To address this challenge, we incorporate task-related public data into the ICL framework while maintaining the DP guarantee. Based on this approach, we propose a private in-context learning algorithm that effectively balances privacy protection and model utility. Through experiments, we demonstrate that our approach significantly improves the utility of private ICL with the assistance of public data. Additionally, we show that our method is robust against membership inference attacks, demonstrating empirical privacy protection."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10932","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:50:50Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:50:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:50:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10946","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10946","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10946","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roberto","familyName":"Morabito","name":"Morabito, Roberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanghan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Guanghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When the Code Autopilot Breaks: Why LLMs Falter in Embedded Machine Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T19:00:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:25:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used to automate software generation in embedded machine learning workflows, yet their outputs often fail silently or behave unpredictably. This article presents an empirical investigation of failure modes in LLM-powered ML pipelines, based on an autopilot framework that orchestrates data preprocessing, model conversion, and on-device inference code generation. We show how prompt format, model behavior, and structural assumptions influence both success rates and failure characteristics, often in ways that standard validation pipelines fail to detect. Our analysis reveals a diverse set of error-prone behaviors, including format-induced misinterpretations and runtime-disruptive code that compiles but breaks downstream. We derive a taxonomy of failure categories and analyze errors across multiple LLMs, highlighting common root causes and systemic fragilities. Though grounded in specific devices, our study reveals broader challenges in LLM-based code generation. We conclude by discussing directions for improving reliability and traceability in LLM-powered embedded ML systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper has been accepted for publication in Computer (IEEE). Upon publication, the copyright will be transferred to IEEE"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10946","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:51:10Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:51:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:51:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10957","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10957","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10957","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Rongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyan","familyName":"Xin","name":"Xin, Ziyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qing","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Qing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiwei","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Ruiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingjia","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Jingjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingbing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Bingbing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhicong","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Zhicong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Digital Landscape of God: Narrative, Visuals and Viewer Engagement of Religious Videos on YouTube"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T19:35:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:26:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-10T09:20:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-13T00:36:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The digital transformation of religious practice has reshaped how billions of people engage with spiritual content, with video-sharing platforms becoming central to contemporary religious communication. Yet HCI research lacks systematic understanding of how narrative and visual elements create meaningful spiritual experiences and foster viewer engagement. We present a mixed-methods study of religious videos on YouTube across major religions, developing taxonomies of narrative frameworks, visual elements, and viewer interaction. Using LLM-assisted analysis, we studied relationships between content characteristics and viewer responses. Religious videos predominantly adopt lecture-style formats with authority-based persuasion strategies, using salvation narratives for guidance. All prefer bright lighting, with Buddhism favoring warm tones and prominent symbols, Judaism preferring indoor settings, and Hinduism emphasizing sacred objects. We identified differentiated patterns of emotional sharing among religious viewers while revealing significant correlations between content characteristics and engagement, particularly regarding AI-generated content. We provide evidence-based guidance for creating inclusive and engaging spiritual media."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This work has been accepted by ICWSM 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10957","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:51:26Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:51:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-13T02:08:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11035","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11035","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11035","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Licheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Licheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cong","familyName":"Zuo","name":"Zuo, Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Free-MAD: Consensus-Free Multi-Agent Debate"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T01:55:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:31:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent debate (MAD) is an emerging approach to improving the reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs). Existing MAD methods rely on multiple rounds of interaction among agents to reach consensus, and the final output is selected by majority voting in the last round. However, this consensus-based design faces several limitations. First, multiple rounds of communication increases token overhead and limits scalability. Second, due to the inherent conformity of LLMs, agents that initially produce correct responses may be influenced by incorrect ones during the debate process, causing error propagation. Third, majority voting introduces randomness and unfairness in the decision-making phase, and can degrade the reasoning performance.\n To address these issues, we propose \\textsc{Free-MAD}, a novel MAD framework that eliminates the need for consensus among agents. \\textsc{Free-MAD} introduces a novel score-based decision mechanism that evaluates the entire debate trajectory rather than relying on the last round only. This mechanism tracks how each agent's reasoning evolves, enabling more accurate and fair outcomes. In addition, \\textsc{Free-MAD} reconstructs the debate phase by introducing anti-conformity, a mechanism that enables agents to mitigate excessive influence from the majority. Experiments on eight benchmark datasets demonstrate that \\textsc{Free-MAD} significantly improves reasoning performance while requiring only a single-round debate and thus reducing token costs. We also show that compared to existing MAD approaches, \\textsc{Free-MAD} exhibits improved robustness in real-world attack scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11035","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:53:15Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:53:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:53:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11056","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11056","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11056","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Ai","name":"Ai, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiguo","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Zhiguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BERT4beam: Large AI Model Enabled Generalized Beamforming Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T02:49:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:32:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-01T13:14:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-02T02:07:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Artificial intelligence (AI) is anticipated to emerge as a pivotal enabler for the forthcoming sixth-generation (6G) wireless communication systems. However, current research efforts regarding large AI models for wireless communications primarily focus on fine-tuning pre-trained large language models (LLMs) for specific tasks. This paper investigates the large-scale AI model designed for beamforming optimization to adapt and generalize to diverse tasks defined by system utilities and scales. We propose a novel framework based on bidirectional encoder representations from transformers (BERT), termed BERT4beam. We aim to formulate the beamforming optimization problem as a token-level sequence learning task, perform tokenization of the channel state information, construct the BERT model, and conduct task-specific pre-training and fine-tuning strategies. Based on the framework, we propose two BERT-based approaches for single-task and multi-task beamforming optimization, respectively. Both approaches are generalizable for varying user scales. Moreover, the former can adapt to varying system utilities and antenna configurations by re-configuring the input and output module of the BERT model, while the latter, termed UBERT, can directly generalize to diverse tasks, due to a finer-grained tokenization strategy. Extensive simulation results demonstrate that the two proposed approaches can achieve near-optimal performance and outperform existing AI models across various beamforming optimization tasks, showcasing strong adaptability and generalizability."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11056","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:53:44Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:53:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:52:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11071","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11071","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11071","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghan","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Jinghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingwen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jingwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dehui","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Dehui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The System Description of CPS Team for Track on Driving with Language of CVPR 2024 Autonomous Grand Challenge"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T03:37:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:32:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This report outlines our approach using vision language model systems for the Driving with Language track of the CVPR 2024 Autonomous Grand Challenge. We have exclusively utilized the DriveLM-nuScenes dataset for training our models. Our systems are built on the LLaVA models, which we enhanced through fine-tuning with the LoRA and DoRA methods. Additionally, we have integrated depth information from open-source depth estimation models to enrich the training and inference processes. For inference, particularly with multiple-choice and yes/no questions, we adopted a Chain-of-Thought reasoning approach to improve the accuracy of the results. This comprehensive methodology enabled us to achieve a top score of 0.7799 on the validation set leaderboard, ranking 1st on the leaderboard."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11071","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:54:05Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:54:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:54:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11076","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11076","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11076","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zibo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhibin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shipeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinjing","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xinjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chendong","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Chendong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingxu","familyName":"Mu","name":"Mu, Bingxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqing","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yuqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiheng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zhiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"She","name":"She, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shu","familyName":"You","name":"You, Shu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanghuan","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Guanghuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rong","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Rong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanchun","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Wanchun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guihai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guihai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SmartSwap: Swap-Based Memory Optimization for LLM Training under Varying Operator Sequences"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T03:55:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:33:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-15T13:51:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-16T00:48:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The increasing size of large language models (LLMs) has led to a surge in memory requirements during training, often exceeding the capacity of high-bandwidth memory (HBM). Swap-based memory optimization incurs neither accuracy loss nor additional end-to-end overhead when effectively overlapped, thus being an attractive solution. However, existing swap methods assume consistent operator sequences, which is impractical in Eager Mode, where operator sequences can vary during change.\n We propose Chameleon, which redesigns the end-to-end process of swap-based memory optimization and is the first work to consider varying operator sequences in Eager Mode. Chameleon (i) introduces a lightweight online profiler to enable continuous profiling for monitoring operator sequences, (ii) generates effective swap policies with limited operator information, and (iii) optimizes the policy execution module for accurate policy application and better performance. Experimental results demonstrate that Chameleon reduces profiling overhead by 84.25%, enables training models up to 4x larger than hardware memory while adapting to changes in operator sequences, improves performance by up to 38.94% compared to recomputation or high-degree parallelism."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to DAC 2026. Previously titled \"Chameleon: Taming Dynamic Operator Sequences for Memory-Intensive LLM Training.\""}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11076","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:54:12Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:54:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-16T02:33:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11079","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11079","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11079","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinwei","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Jinwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qizhen","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Qizhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinghui","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yinghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lifan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Lifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiyou","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Weiyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyuan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lewei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Lewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Jingsong","name":"Jingsong, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Difficulty-Aware Agentic Orchestration for Query-Specific Multi-Agent Workflows"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T03:57:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T02:48:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T13:32:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:52:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-11T12:33:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:35:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-08T06:37:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-09T02:14:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-13T16:13:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-02-16T01:50:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Model (LLM)-based agentic systems have shown strong capabilities across various tasks. However, existing multi-agent frameworks often rely on static or task-level workflows, which either over-process simple queries or underperform on complex ones, while also neglecting the efficiency-performance trade-offs across heterogeneous LLMs. To address these limitations, we propose Difficulty-Aware Agentic Orchestration (DAAO), which can dynamically generate query-specific multi-agent workflows guided by predicted query difficulty. DAAO comprises three interdependent modules: a variational autoencoder (VAE) for difficulty estimation, a modular operator allocator, and a cost- and performance-aware LLM router. A self-adjusting policy updates difficulty estimates based on workflow success, enabling simpler workflows for easy queries and more complex strategies for harder ones. Experiments on six benchmarks demonstrate that DAAO surpasses prior multi-agent systems in both accuracy and inference efficiency, validating its effectiveness for adaptive, difficulty-aware reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to WWW2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11079","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:54:16Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:54:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-16T02:59:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11112","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11112","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11112","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Baqer","familyName":"Mollah","name":"Mollah, Muhammad Baqer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honggang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Honggang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hua","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Hua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Modal Sensing Aided mmWave Beamforming for V2V Communications with Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Emerging Technologies (cs.ET)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T06:03:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:35:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Beamforming techniques are utilized in millimeter wave (mmWave) communication to address the inherent path loss limitation, thereby establishing and maintaining reliable connections. However, adopting standard defined beamforming approach in highly dynamic vehicular environments often incurs high beam training overheads and reduces the available airtime for communications, which is mainly due to exchanging pilot signals and exhaustive beam measurements. To this end, we present a multi-modal sensing and fusion learning framework as a potential alternative solution to reduce such overheads. In this framework, we first extract the features individually from the visual and GPS coordinates sensing modalities by modality specific encoders, and subsequently fuse the multimodal features to obtain predicted top-k beams so that the best line-of-sight links can be proactively established. To show the generalizability of the proposed framework, we perform a comprehensive experiment in four different vehicle-to-vehicle (V2V) scenarios from real-world multi-modal sensing and communication dataset. From the experiment, we observe that the proposed framework achieves up to 77.58% accuracy on predicting top-15 beams correctly, outperforms single modalities, incurs roughly as low as 2.32 dB average power loss, and considerably reduces the beam searching space overheads by 76.56% for top-15 beams with respect to standard defined approach."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 Pages, Accepted to present at 2025 IEEE Global Communications Conference (GLOBECOM), Taipei, Taiwan"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11112","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:55:01Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:55:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:55:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11134","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11134","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11134","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaang","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Jiaang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shenglin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Shenglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyou","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Shiyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingyu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Dingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kangjin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kangjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenzhi","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Chenzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinghao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Yinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qin","familyName":"Hua","name":"Hua, Qin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanwen","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Hanwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenchao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Wenchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongqing","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Dongqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangtao","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Guangtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guodong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Guodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liping","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Liping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GFS: A Preemption-aware Scheduling Framework for GPU Clusters with Predictive Spot Instance Management"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T07:05:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:36:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The surge in large language models (LLMs) has fundamentally reshaped the landscape of GPU usage patterns, creating an urgent need for more efficient management strategies. While cloud providers employ spot instances to reduce costs for low-priority (LP) tasks, existing schedulers still grapple with high eviction rates and lengthy queuing times. To address these limitations, we present GFS, a novel preemptive scheduling framework that enhances service-level objective (SLO) compliance for high-priority (HP) tasks while minimizing preemptions to LP tasks. Firstly, GFS utilizes a lightweight forecasting model that predicts GPU demand among different tenants, enabling proactive resource management. Secondly, GFS employs a dynamic allocation mechanism to adjust the spot quota for LP tasks with guaranteed durations. Lastly, GFS incorporates a preemptive scheduling policy that prioritizes HP tasks while minimizing the impact on LP tasks. We demonstrate the effectiveness of GFS through both real-world implementation and simulations. The results show that GFS reduces eviction rates by 33.0\\%, and cuts queuing delays by 44.1\\% for LP tasks. Furthermore, GFS enhances the GPU allocation rate by up to 22.8\\% in real production clusters. In a production cluster of more than 10,000 GPUs, GFS yields roughly \\$459,715 in monthly benefits."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper has been accepted to the 31st ACM International Conference on Architectural Support for Programming Languages and Operating Systems (ASPLOS 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11134","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:55:32Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:55:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:55:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11141","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11141","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11141","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyao","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Shiyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xijia","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Xijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingkang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yingkang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junxiao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Junxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhexin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhexin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biplab","familyName":"Sikdar","name":"Sikdar, Biplab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongning","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hongning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minlie","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Minlie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Smiley Turns Hostile: Interpreting How Emojis Trigger LLMs' Toxicity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T07:21:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:36:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Emojis are globally used non-verbal cues in digital communication, and extensive research has examined how large language models (LLMs) understand and utilize emojis across contexts. While usually associated with friendliness or playfulness, it is observed that emojis may trigger toxic content generation in LLMs. Motivated by such a observation, we aim to investigate: (1) whether emojis can clearly enhance the toxicity generation in LLMs and (2) how to interpret this phenomenon. We begin with a comprehensive exploration of emoji-triggered LLM toxicity generation by automating the construction of prompts with emojis to subtly express toxic intent. Experiments across 5 mainstream languages on 7 famous LLMs along with jailbreak tasks demonstrate that prompts with emojis could easily induce toxicity generation. To understand this phenomenon, we conduct model-level interpretations spanning semantic cognition, sequence generation and tokenization, suggesting that emojis can act as a heterogeneous semantic channel to bypass the safety mechanisms. To pursue deeper insights, we further probe the pre-training corpus and uncover potential correlation between the emoji-related data polution with the toxicity generation behaviors. Supplementary materials provide our implementation code and data. (Warning: This paper contains potentially sensitive contents)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11141","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:55:42Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:55:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:55:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11155","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11155","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11155","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Santhosh G","familyName":"S","name":"S, Santhosh G","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saurav","familyName":"Prakash","name":"Prakash, Saurav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Balaraman","familyName":"Ravindran","name":"Ravindran, Balaraman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AQUA: Attention via QUery mAgnitudes for Memory and Compute Efficient Inference in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T08:20:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:38:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The quadratic complexity of the attention mechanism remains a fundamental barrier to scaling Large Language Models (LLMs) to longer contexts, creating a critical bottleneck in both computation and memory. To address this, we introduce AQUA (Attention via QUery mAgnitudes) a novel and versatile approximation strategy that significantly reduces the cost of attention with a graceful performance trade-off. Our method operates in two phases: an efficient offline step where we compute a universal, language agnostic projection matrix via SVD on a calibration dataset, and an online inference step where we project query and key vectors and dynamically select a sparse subset of dimensions based on the query's magnitude. We provide a formal theoretical analysis of AQUA, establishing the break-even point at which it becomes more computationally efficient than standard attention. Our empirical evaluations on state-of-the-art models like Llama-3.1-8B demonstrate that a 25% reduction in the attention dot-product computation can be achieved with a statistically insignificant impact on performance across a wide range of benchmarks. We further showcase the versatility of AQUA by demonstrating its ability to synergistically accelerate existing token eviction methods like H2O and to directly reduce KV-cache memory size. By offering a controllable knob to balance efficiency and accuracy, AQUA provides a practical and powerful tool for making large-scale LLM inference more accessible and sustainable."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11155","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:56:02Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:56:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:56:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11165","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11165","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11165","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Waikit","familyName":"Xiu","name":"Xiu, Waikit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingchen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiying","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Traffic-MLLM: Curiosity-Regularized Supervised Learning for Traffic Scenario Case-Based Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T08:53:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:38:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-07T14:12:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:45:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"For safe and robust autonomous driving, decision-making systems must effectively leverage past experiences to handle the inherent long-tail of traffic scenarios. Case-Based Reasoning (CBR) provides a natural paradigm for this by adapting solutions from prior cases. However, in complex and dynamic traffic environments, traditional CBR methods struggle to effectively abstract and adapt knowledge under uncertainty. Meanwhile, although multimodal large language models (MLLMs) exhibit strong perceptual and linguistic capabilities, their reasoning behavior often relies on empirical pattern fitting, limiting robustness under distribution shift and long-tail scenarios. We propose Traffic-MLLM, a retrieval-free neural case modeling framework for multimodal traffic reasoning. Instead of performing explicit case retrieval at inference time, Traffic-MLLM learns a structured and generalizable case space directly during training. To support this learning process, we construct a multi-source case base by integrating dynamic traffic videos and large-scale static visual question-answering data, serving as a unified training substrate for learning structured case representations. To further improve representation quality near knowledge boundaries, we introduce a curiosity-driven refinement mechanism based on Random Network Distillation (RND), encouraging the model to internalize cross-case structural regularities rather than surface correlations. Experiments on the SUTD-TrafficQA and DriveQA benchmarks demonstrate consistent improvements in dynamic reasoning, regulatory understanding, and cross-domain transfer. Traffic-MLLM achieves 50.8% accuracy on SUTD-TrafficQA, 74.8% on the CARLA-based DriveQA split, and 83.1% on the real-world Mapillary split, indicating that representation-level case-space refinement provides an effective alternative to explicit retrieval for scalable multimodal case adaptation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11165","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:56:16Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:56:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:49:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11183","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11183","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11183","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emmanouil","familyName":"Karystinaios","name":"Karystinaios, Emmanouil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WeaveMuse: An Open Agentic System for Multimodal Music Understanding and Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T09:29:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:39:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agentic AI has been standardized in industry as a practical paradigm for coordinating specialized models and tools to solve complex multimodal tasks. In this work, we present WeaveMuse, a multi-agent system for music understanding, symbolic composition, and audio synthesis. Each specialist agent interprets user requests, derives machine-actionable requirements (modalities, formats, constraints), and validates its own outputs, while a manager agent selects and sequences tools, mediates user interaction, and maintains state across turns. The system is extendable and deployable either locally, using quantization and inference strategies to fit diverse hardware budgets, or via the HFApi to preserve free community access to open models. Beyond out-of-the-box use, the system emphasizes controllability and adaptation through constraint schemas, structured decoding, policy-based inference, and parameter-efficient adapters or distilled variants that tailor models to MIR tasks. A central design goal is to facilitate intermodal interaction across text, symbolic notation and visualization, and audio, enabling analysis-synthesis-render loops and addressing cross-format constraints. The framework aims to democratize, implement, and make accessible MIR tools by supporting interchangeable open-source models of various sizes, flexible memory management, and reproducible deployment paths."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at Large Language Models for Music \u0026amp; Audio Workshop (LLM4MA) 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11183","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:56:41Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:56:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:56:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11233","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11233","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11233","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emil","familyName":"Malmsten","name":"Malmsten, Emil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wendelin","familyName":"Böhmer","name":"Böhmer, Wendelin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TransZero: Parallel Tree Expansion in MuZero using Transformer Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T12:20:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:43:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present TransZero, a model-based reinforcement learning algorithm that removes the sequential bottleneck in Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS). Unlike MuZero, which constructs its search tree step by step using a recurrent dynamics model, TransZero employs a transformer-based network to generate multiple latent future states simultaneously. Combined with the Mean-Variance Constrained (MVC) evaluator that eliminates dependence on inherently sequential visitation counts, our approach enables the parallel expansion of entire subtrees during planning. Experiments in MiniGrid and LunarLander show that TransZero achieves up to an eleven-fold speedup in wall-clock time compared to MuZero while maintaining sample efficiency. These results demonstrate that parallel tree construction can substantially accelerate model-based reinforcement learning, bringing real-time decision-making in complex environments closer to practice. The code is publicly available on GitHub."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to BNAIC/BeNeLearn 2025. 15 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11233","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:57:50Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:57:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:57:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11243","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11243","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11243","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haozhen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haozhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruide","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruide","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongqing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Rongqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Synesthesia of Machines (SoM)-Empowered Wireless Image Transmission over Complex Dynamic Channel"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T12:35:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:43:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Wireless image transmission underpins diverse networked intelligent services and becomes an increasingly critical issue. Existing works have shown that deep learning-based joint source-channel coding (JSCC) is an effective framework to balance image transmission fidelity and data overhead. However, these studies oversimplify the communication system as a mere pipeline with noise, failing to account for the complex dynamics of wireless channels and concrete physical-layer transmission process. To address these limitations, we propose a Synesthesia of Machines (SoM)-empowered Dynamic Channel Adaptive Transmission (DCAT) scheme, designed for practical implementation in real communication scenarios. Building upon the Swin Transformer backbone, our DCAT scheme demonstrates robust adaptability to time-selective fading and channel aging effects by effectively utilizing the physical-layer transmission characteristics of wireless channels. Comprehensive experimental results confirm that DCAT consistently achieves superior performance compared with JSCC baseline approaches across all conditions. Furthermore, our neural network architecture demonstrates high scalability due to its interpretable design, offering substantial potential for cost-efficient deployment in practical applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11243","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:58:04Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:58:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:58:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11254","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11254","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11254","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengping","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Shengping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuyan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chuyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From PowerSGD to PowerSGD+: Low-Rank Gradient Compression for Distributed Optimization with Convergence Guarantees"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T12:54:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:43:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Low-rank gradient compression methods, such as PowerSGD, have gained attention in communication-efficient distributed optimization. However, the convergence guarantees of PowerSGD remain unclear, particularly in stochastic settings. In this paper, we show that PowerSGD does not always converge to the optimal solution and provide a clear counterexample to support this finding. To address this, we introduce PowerSGD+, which periodically updates the projection subspace via singular value decomposition, ensuring that it remains aligned with the optimal subspace. We prove that PowerSGD+ converges under standard assumptions and validate its effectiveness through empirical evaluation on large language model tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11254","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:58:21Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:58:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:58:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11255","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11255","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11255","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hayat","familyName":"Rajani","name":"Rajani, Hayat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nuno","familyName":"Gracias","name":"Gracias, Nuno","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rafael","familyName":"Garcia","name":"Garcia, Rafael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huigang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huigang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Geometrically Consistent Matching Framework for Side-Scan Sonar Mapping"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Detectors (physics.ins-det)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T12:54:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:43:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T16:27:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:58:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-21T15:36:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-24T01:43:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Robust matching of side-scan sonar imagery remains a fundamental challenge in seafloor mapping due to view-dependent backscatter, shadows, and geometric distortion. This paper proposes a novel matching framework that combines physical decoupling and geometric consistency to enhance correspondence accuracy and consistency across viewpoints. A multi-branch network, derived from the Lambertian reflection model, decomposes raw sonar images into seabed reflectivity, terrain elevation, and acoustic path loss. The reflectivity map, serving as a stable matching domain, is used in conjunction with a training-free matching pipeline combining SuperPoint and MINIMA-LightGlue. Geometry-aware outlier rejection leverages both terrain elevation and its physically derived shadow map to further remove mismatches in acoustically occluded and topographically inconsistent regions, thereby improving registration accuracy. Quantitative and visual evaluations against traditional, CNN-, and Transformer-based state-of-the-art methods demonstrate that our method achieves lower matching error, higher geometric consistency, and greater robustness to viewpoint variations. The proposed approach provides a data-efficient, physically interpretable solution for high-precision side-scan sonar image matching in complex seafloor environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11255","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:58:22Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:58:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-24T02:48:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11311","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11311","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11311","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingchen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zi-Yu","familyName":"Khoo","name":"Khoo, Zi-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingtan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jingtan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prompts to Proxies: Emulating Human Preferences via a Compact LLM Ensemble"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T15:08:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:46:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-28T19:39:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-30T01:04:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models are increasingly used as proxies for human subjects in social science research, yet external validity requires that synthetic agents faithfully reflect the preferences of target human populations. We introduce *preference reconstruction theory*, a framework that formalizes preference alignment as a representation learning problem: constructing a functional basis of proxy agents and recovering population preferences through weighted aggregation. We implement this via *Prompts to Proxies* ($\\texttt{P2P}$), a modular two-stage system. Stage 1 uses structured prompting with entropy-based adaptive sampling to construct a diverse agent pool spanning the latent preference space. Stage 2 employs L1-regularized regression to select a compact ensemble whose aggregate response distributions align with observed data from the target population. $\\texttt{P2P}$ requires no finetuning and no access to sensitive demographic data, incurring only API inference costs. We validate the approach on 14 waves of the American Trends Panel, achieving an average test MSE of 0.014 across diverse topics at approximately 0.8 USD per survey. We additionally test it on the World Values Survey, demonstrating its potential to generalize across locales. When stress-tested against an SFT-aligned baseline, $\\texttt{P2P}$ achieves competitive performance using less than 3% of the training data."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11311","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:59:40Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:59:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-30T03:41:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11413","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11413","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11413","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Grigori","familyName":"Fursin","name":"Fursin, Grigori","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Altunay","name":"Altunay, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Framing AI System Benchmarking as a Learning Task: FlexBench and the Open MLPerf Dataset"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T20:02:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:52:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing AI system benchmarks such as MLPerf often struggle to keep pace with the rapidly evolving AI landscape, making it difficult to support informed deployment, optimization, and co-design decisions for AI systems. We suggest that benchmarking itself can be framed as an AI task - one in which models are continuously evaluated and optimized across diverse datasets, software, and hardware, using key metrics such as accuracy, latency, throughput, energy consumption, and cost. To support this perspective, we present FlexBench: a modular extension of the MLPerf LLM inference benchmark, integrated with HuggingFace and designed to provide relevant and actionable insights. Benchmarking results and metadata are collected into an Open MLPerf Dataset, which can be collaboratively curated, extended, and leveraged for predictive modeling and feature engineering. We successfully validated the FlexBench concept through MLPerf Inference submissions, including evaluations of DeepSeek R1 and LLaMA 3.3 on commodity servers. The broader objective is to enable practitioners to make cost-effective AI deployment decisions that reflect their available resources, requirements, and constraints."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11413","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:02:06Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:02:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:02:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11465","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11465","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11465","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amirhossein","familyName":"Abaskohi","name":"Abaskohi, Amirhossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raymond","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Raymond","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chuyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shafiq","familyName":"Joty","name":"Joty, Shafiq","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppe","familyName":"Carenini","name":"Carenini, Giuseppe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CEMTM: Contextual Embedding-based Multimodal Topic Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T23:07:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:55:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-05T16:11:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:00:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce CEMTM, a context-enhanced multimodal topic model designed to infer coherent and interpretable topic structures from both short and long documents containing text and images. CEMTM builds on fine-tuned large vision language models (LVLMs) to obtain contextualized embeddings, and employs a distributional attention mechanism to weight token-level contributions to topic inference. A reconstruction objective aligns topic-based representations with the document embedding, encouraging semantic consistency across modalities. Unlike existing approaches, CEMTM can process multiple images per document without repeated encoding and maintains interpretability through explicit word-topic and document-topic distributions. Extensive experiments on six multimodal benchmarks show that CEMTM consistently outperforms unimodal and multimodal baselines, achieving a remarkable average LLM score of 2.61. Further analysis shows its effectiveness in downstream few-shot retrieval and its ability to capture visually grounded semantics in complex domains such as scientific articles."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11465","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:03:19Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:03:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:49:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11466","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11466","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11466","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujian","familyName":"Gan","name":"Gan, Yujian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanni","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yanni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juntao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Juntao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Massimo","familyName":"Poesio","name":"Poesio, Massimo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving LLMs' Learning for Coreference Resolution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T23:08:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:55:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Coreference Resolution (CR) is crucial for many NLP tasks, but existing LLMs struggle with hallucination and under-performance. In this paper, we investigate the limitations of existing LLM-based approaches to CR-specifically the Question-Answering (QA) Template and Document Template methods and propose two novel techniques: Reversed Training with Joint Inference and Iterative Document Generation. Our experiments show that Reversed Training improves the QA Template method, while Iterative Document Generation eliminates hallucinations in the generated source text and boosts coreference resolution. Integrating these methods and techniques offers an effective and robust solution to LLM-based coreference resolution."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11466","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:03:20Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:03:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:03:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11480","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11480","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11480","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amir","familyName":"Taherin","name":"Taherin, Amir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juyi","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Juyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arash","familyName":"Akbari","name":"Akbari, Arash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arman","familyName":"Akbari","name":"Akbari, Arman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Pu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiwei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Weiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Kaeli","name":"Kaeli, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanzhi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yanzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cross-Platform Scaling of Vision-Language-Action Models from Edge to Cloud GPUs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Emerging Technologies (cs.ET)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:56:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-26T15:57:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:36:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have emerged as powerful generalist policies for robotic control, yet their performance scaling across model architectures and hardware platforms, as well as their associated power budgets, remain poorly understood. This work presents an evaluation of five representative VLA models -- spanning state-of-the-art baselines and two newly proposed architectures -- targeting edge and datacenter GPU platforms. Using the LIBERO benchmark, we measure accuracy alongside system-level metrics, including latency, throughput, and peak memory usage, under varying edge power constraints and high-performance datacenter GPU configurations. Our results identify distinct scaling trends: (1) architectural choices, such as action tokenization and model backbone size, strongly influence throughput and memory footprint; (2) power-constrained edge devices exhibit non-linear performance degradation, with some configurations matching or exceeding older datacenter GPUs; and (3) high-throughput variants can be achieved without significant accuracy loss. These findings provide actionable insights when selecting and optimizing VLAs across a range of deployment constraints. Our work challenges current assumptions about the superiority of datacenter hardware for robotic inference."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in the Asilomar Conference on Signals, Systems, and Computers 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11480","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:03:40Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:03:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:34:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11496","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11496","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11496","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabrycio Leite Nakano","familyName":"Almada","name":"Almada, Fabrycio Leite Nakano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kauan Divino Pouso","familyName":"Mariano","name":"Mariano, Kauan Divino Pouso","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maykon Adriell","familyName":"Dutra","name":"Dutra, Maykon Adriell","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor Emanuel da Silva","familyName":"Monteiro","name":"Monteiro, Victor Emanuel da Silva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juliana Resplande Sant'Anna","familyName":"Gomes","name":"Gomes, Juliana Resplande Sant'Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arlindo Rodrigues Galvão","familyName":"Filho","name":"Filho, Arlindo Rodrigues Galvão","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anderson da Silva","familyName":"Soares","name":"Soares, Anderson da Silva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AKCIT-FN at CheckThat! 2025: Switching Fine-Tuned SLMs and LLM Prompting for Multilingual Claim Normalization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T01:19:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:57:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Claim normalization, the transformation of informal social media posts into concise, self-contained statements, is a crucial step in automated fact-checking pipelines. This paper details our submission to the CLEF-2025 CheckThat! Task~2, which challenges systems to perform claim normalization across twenty languages, divided into thirteen supervised (high-resource) and seven zero-shot (no training data) tracks.\n Our approach, leveraging fine-tuned Small Language Models (SLMs) for supervised languages and Large Language Model (LLM) prompting for zero-shot scenarios, achieved podium positions (top three) in fifteen of the twenty languages. Notably, this included second-place rankings in eight languages, five of which were among the seven designated zero-shot languages, underscoring the effectiveness of our LLM-based zero-shot strategy. For Portuguese, our initial development language, our system achieved an average METEOR score of 0.5290, ranking third. All implementation artifacts, including inference, training, evaluation scripts, and prompt configurations, are publicly available at https://github.com/ju-resplande/checkthat2025_normalization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11496","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:04:02Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:04:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:04:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11514","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11514","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11514","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengxiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhengxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiling","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weiling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Panagiotis","familyName":"Kaliosis","name":"Kaliosis, Panagiotis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Owen","familyName":"Rambow","name":"Rambow, Owen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Susan E.","familyName":"Brennan","name":"Brennan, Susan E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LVLMs are Bad at Overhearing Human Referential Communication"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T02:03:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:58:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-24T00:25:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:13:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"During spontaneous conversations, speakers collaborate on novel referring expressions, which they can then re-use in subsequent conversations. Understanding such referring expressions is an important ability for an embodied agent, so that it can carry out tasks in the real world. This requires integrating and understanding language, vision, and conversational interaction. We study the capabilities of seven state-of-the-art Large Vision Language Models (LVLMs) as overhearers to a corpus of spontaneous conversations between pairs of human discourse participants engaged in a collaborative object-matching task. We find that such a task remains challenging for current LVLMs and they all fail to show a consistent performance improvement as they overhear more conversations from the same discourse participants repeating the same task for multiple rounds. We release our corpus and code for reproducibility and to facilitate future research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 (Main)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11514","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:04:28Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:04:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T02:11:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11524","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11524","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11524","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengbing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chengbing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhicheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianhao","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Tianhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keqin","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Keqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuli","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Fuli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tat-Seng","familyName":"Chua","name":"Chua, Tat-Seng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Decoding in Latent Spaces for Efficient Inference in LLM-based Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T02:30:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:58:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fine-tuning large language models (LLMs) for recommendation in a generative manner has delivered promising results, but encounters significant inference overhead due to autoregressive decoding in the language space. This work explores bypassing language-space decoding by directly matching candidate items with the LLM's internal thought representations in the latent space, eliminating the time-consuming autoregressive process to reduce computational costs. Towards this, we introduce Light Latent-space Decoding (L2D), an effective and efficient latent-space decoding method. L2D represents user-preferred items by using the hidden states of test sequences reflecting the LLM's internal thought, and obtains candidate item representations from the hidden states of training sequences labeled with the corresponding candidate items. It then matches the two types of representations to decode items, achieving latent-space decoding. In this way, it enables efficient decoding without altering the LLM's generative tuning paradigm, thereby preserving performance. Extensive empirical results demonstrate that L2D is more than 10x faster than language-space decoding while maintaining or enhancing performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication in EMNLP'25"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11524","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:04:42Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:04:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:04:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11548","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11548","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11548","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujing","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yujing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ling","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tongxiao","familyName":"Ruan","name":"Ruan, Tongxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manni","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Manni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Auxiliary Reasoning Unleashes GUI Grounding in VLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T03:28:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:00:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-10T01:13:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-11T00:19:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Graphical user interface (GUI) grounding is a fundamental task for building GUI agents. However, general vision-language models (VLMs) struggle with this task due to a lack of specific optimization. We identify a key gap in this paper: while VLMs exhibit significant latent grounding potential, as demonstrated by their performance measured by Pointing Game, they underperform when tasked with outputting explicit coordinates. To address this discrepancy and bypass the high data and annotation costs of current fine-tuning approaches, we propose three zero-shot auxiliary reasoning methods. By providing explicit spatial cues such as axes, grids and labeled intersections as part of the input image, these methods enable VLMs to better articulate their implicit spatial understanding capabilities. We evaluate these methods on four GUI grounding benchmarks across seven open-source and proprietary VLMs. Experimental results show substantial gains from auxiliary reasoning. Mark-Grid Scaffold boosts Gemini-3.1-Pro from 11.72\\% under direct inference to 95.20\\% on ScreenSpot-v2, achieves state-of-the-art performance on ScreenSpot, and approaches the strongest fine-tuned methods on ScreenSpot-v2 and UI-I2E-Bench. Our code is available at https://github.com/liweim/AuxiliaryReasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11548","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:05:16Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:05:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-11T02:24:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11569","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11569","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11569","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofang","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xiaofang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianze","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Tianze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junfei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Junfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinlong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xinlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"D$^2$HScore: Reasoning-Aware Hallucination Detection via Semantic Breadth and Depth Analysis in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T04:28:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:01:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable success, their practical application is often hindered by the generation of non-factual content, which is called \"hallucination\". Ensuring the reliability of LLMs' outputs is a critical challenge, particularly in high-stakes domains such as finance, security, and healthcare. In this work, we revisit hallucination detection from the perspective of model architecture and generation dynamics. Leveraging the multi-layer structure and autoregressive decoding process of LLMs, we decompose hallucination signals into two complementary dimensions: the semantic breadth of token representations within each layer, and the semantic depth of core concepts as they evolve across layers. Based on this insight, we propose \\textbf{D$^2$HScore (Dispersion and Drift-based Hallucination Score)}, a training-free and label-free framework that jointly measures: (1) \\textbf{Intra-Layer Dispersion}, which quantifies the semantic diversity of token representations within each layer; and (2) \\textbf{Inter-Layer Drift}, which tracks the progressive transformation of key token representations across layers. To ensure drift reflects the evolution of meaningful semantics rather than noisy or redundant tokens, we guide token selection using attention signals. By capturing both the horizontal and vertical dynamics of representation during inference, D$^2$HScore provides an interpretable and lightweight proxy for hallucination detection. Extensive experiments across five open-source LLMs and five widely used benchmarks demonstrate that D$^2$HScore consistently outperforms existing training-free baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11569","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:05:46Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:05:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:05:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11572","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11572","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11572","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuan","familyName":"Bui","name":"Bui, Tuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"An","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Phat","familyName":"Thai","name":"Thai, Phat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minh","familyName":"Hua","name":"Hua, Minh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ngan Pham L.","familyName":"N.","name":"N., Ngan Pham L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ngan Pham T.","familyName":"B.","name":"B., Ngan Pham T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dung","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Dung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Long","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thanh-Tung","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Thanh-Tung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thang","familyName":"Bui","name":"Bui, Thang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tho","familyName":"Quan","name":"Quan, Tho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Formal Reasoning for Intelligent QA Systems: A Case Study in the Educational Domain"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T04:34:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:02:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3746274.3760392","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning is essential for closed-domain QA systems in which procedural correctness and policy compliance are critical. While large language models (LLMs) have shown strong performance on many reasoning tasks, recent work reveals that their reasoning traces are often unfaithful - serving more as plausible justifications than as causally grounded derivations. Efforts to combine LLMs with symbolic engines (e.g., Prover9, Z3) have improved reliability but remain limited to static forms of logic, struggling with dynamic, state-based reasoning such as multi-step progressions and conditional transitions.\n In this paper, we propose MCFR (Model Checking for Formal Reasoning), a neuro-symbolic framework that integrates LLMs with model checking to support property verification. MCFR translates natural language into formal specifications and verifies them over transition models. To support evaluation, we introduce EduMC-QA, a benchmark dataset grounded in real academic procedures. Our results show that MCFR improves reasoning faithfulness and interpretability, offering a viable path toward verifiable QA in high-stakes closed-domain applications. In addition to evaluating MCFR, we compare its performance with state-of-the-art LLMs such as ChatGPT, DeepSeek, and Claude to contextualize its effectiveness."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at the 2nd ACM Workshop in AI-powered Question \u0026amp; Answering Systems (AIQAM '25), co-located with ACM Multimedia 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11572","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-16T03:05:50Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:05:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:05:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11575","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11575","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11575","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ching","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Ching","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yidan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Yidan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Defu","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Defu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeehyun","familyName":"Hwang","name":"Hwang, Jeehyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haixin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacheng","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Jiacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen-Chih","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Wen-Chih","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tien-Fu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tien-Fu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Survey of Reasoning and Agentic Systems in Time Series with Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T04:39:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:02:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-02T22:14:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-04T02:07:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-10T07:42:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-11T00:35:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Time series reasoning treats time as a first-class axis and incorporates intermediate evidence directly into the answer. This survey defines the problem and organizes the literature by reasoning topology with three families: direct reasoning in one step, linear chain reasoning with explicit intermediates, and branch-structured reasoning that explores, revises, and aggregates. The topology is crossed with the main objectives of the field, including traditional time series analysis, explanation and understanding, causal inference and decision making, and time series generation, while a compact tag set spans these axes and captures decomposition and verification, ensembling, tool use, knowledge access, multimodality, agent loops, and LLM alignment regimes. Methods and systems are reviewed across domains, showing what each topology enables and where it breaks down in faithfulness or robustness, along with curated datasets, benchmarks, and resources that support study and deployment (https://github.com/blacksnail789521/Time-Series-Reasoning-Survey). Evaluation practices that keep evidence visible and temporally aligned are highlighted, and guidance is distilled on matching topology to uncertainty, grounding with observable artifacts, planning for shift and streaming, and treating cost and latency as design budgets. We emphasize that reasoning structures must balance capacity for grounding and self-correction against computational cost and reproducibility, while future progress will likely depend on benchmarks that tie reasoning quality to utility and on closed-loop testbeds that trade off cost and risk under shift-aware, streaming, and long-horizon settings. Taken together, these directions mark a shift from narrow accuracy toward reliability at scale, enabling systems that not only analyze but also understand, explain, and act on dynamic worlds with traceable evidence and credible outcomes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to Transactions on Machine Learning Research (TMLR)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11575","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:05:54Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:05:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-11T02:24:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11617","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11617","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11617","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaoran","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chaoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijian","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Zijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"EnTe","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, EnTe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shubo","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Shubo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinghongbing","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Qinghongbing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaobo","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhaobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Long","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AssemMate: Graph-Based LLM for Robotic Assembly Assistance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T06:17:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:04:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-01T05:58:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:02:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Model (LLM)-based robotic assembly assistance has gained significant research attention. It requires the injection of domain-specific knowledge to guide the assembly process through natural language interaction with humans. Despite some progress, existing methods represent knowledge in the form of natural language text. Due to the long context and redundant content, they struggle to meet the robots' requirements for real-time and precise reasoning. In order to bridge this gap, we present AssemMate, which utilizes the graph\\textemdash a concise and accurate form of knowledge representation\\textemdash as input. This graph-based LLM enables knowledge graph question answering (KGQA), supporting human-robot interaction and assembly task planning for specific products. Beyond interactive QA, AssemMate also supports sensing stacked scenes and executing grasping to assist with assembly. Specifically, a self-supervised Graph Convolutional Network (GCN) encodes knowledge graph entities and relations into a latent space and aligns them with LLM's representation, enabling the LLM to understand graph information. In addition, a vision-enhanced strategy is employed to address stacked scenes in grasping. Through training and evaluation, AssemMate outperforms existing methods, achieving 6.4\\% higher accuracy, 3 times faster inference, and 28 times shorter context length, while demonstrating strong generalization ability on random graphs. And our approach further demonstrates superiority through robotic grasping experiments in both simulated and real-world settings. More details can be found on the project page: https://github.com/cristina304/AssemMate.git"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11617","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:06:52Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:06:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:42:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11619","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11619","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11619","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Spandan","familyName":"Anaokar","name":"Anaokar, Spandan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shrey","familyName":"Ganatra","name":"Ganatra, Shrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harshvivek","familyName":"Kashid","name":"Kashid, Harshvivek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Swapnil","familyName":"Bhattacharyya","name":"Bhattacharyya, Swapnil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shruti","familyName":"Nair","name":"Nair, Shruti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reshma","familyName":"Sekhar","name":"Sekhar, Reshma","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siddharth","familyName":"Manohar","name":"Manohar, Siddharth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Hemrajani","name":"Hemrajani, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pushpak","familyName":"Bhattacharyya","name":"Bhattacharyya, Pushpak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HalluDetect: Detecting, Mitigating, and Benchmarking Hallucinations in Conversational Systems in the Legal Domain"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T06:23:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:04:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-12T05:15:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:52:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are widely used in industry but remain prone to hallucinations, limiting their reliability in critical applications. This work addresses hallucination reduction in consumer grievance chatbots built using LLaMA 3.1 8B Instruct, a compact model frequently used in industry. We develop HalluDetect, an LLM-based hallucination detection system that achieves an F1 score of 68.92% outperforming baseline detectors by 22.47%. Benchmarking five hallucination mitigation architectures, we find that out of them, AgentBot minimizes hallucinations to 0.4159 per turn while maintaining the highest token accuracy (96.13%), making it the most effective mitigation strategy. Our findings provide a scalable framework for hallucination mitigation, demonstrating that optimized inference strategies can significantly improve factual accuracy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages + references + appendix, 3 figures, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11619","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:06:55Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:06:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:21:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11628","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11628","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11628","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Chang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanhuiyi","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Yuanhuiyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaobo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shaobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaixin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kaixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SpeCa: Accelerating Diffusion Transformers with Speculative Feature Caching"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T06:46:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:05:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3746027.3755331","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models have revolutionized high-fidelity image and video synthesis, yet their computational demands remain prohibitive for real-time applications. These models face two fundamental challenges: strict temporal dependencies preventing parallelization, and computationally intensive forward passes required at each denoising step. Drawing inspiration from speculative decoding in large language models, we present SpeCa, a novel 'Forecast-then-verify' acceleration framework that effectively addresses both limitations. SpeCa's core innovation lies in introducing Speculative Sampling to diffusion models, predicting intermediate features for subsequent timesteps based on fully computed reference timesteps. Our approach implements a parameter-free verification mechanism that efficiently evaluates prediction reliability, enabling real-time decisions to accept or reject each prediction while incurring negligible computational overhead. Furthermore, SpeCa introduces sample-adaptive computation allocation that dynamically modulates resources based on generation complexity, allocating reduced computation for simpler samples while preserving intensive processing for complex instances. Experiments demonstrate 6.34x acceleration on FLUX with minimal quality degradation (5.5% drop), 7.3x speedup on DiT while preserving generation fidelity, and 79.84% VBench score at 6.1x acceleration for HunyuanVideo. The verification mechanism incurs minimal overhead (1.67%-3.5% of full inference costs), establishing a new paradigm for efficient diffusion model inference while maintaining generation quality even at aggressive acceleration ratios. Our codes have been released in Github: \\textbf{https://github.com/Shenyi-Z/Cache4Diffusion}"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 9 figures, ACM Multimedia 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11628","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-16T03:07:08Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:07:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:07:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11629","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11629","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11629","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chentao","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Chentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojun","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiaojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoned Safety Alignment: Ensuring Jailbreak Defense via Answer-Then-Check"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T06:47:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:05:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-06T02:11:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-09T00:17:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) continue to advance in capabilities, ensuring their safety against jailbreak attacks remains a critical challenge. In this paper, we introduce a novel safety alignment approach called Answer-Then-Check, which enhances LLM robustness against malicious prompts by applying thinking ability to mitigate jailbreaking problems before producing a final answer to the user. Our method enables models to answer the question in their thoughts directly and then critically evaluate its safety before deciding whether to provide it. To implement this approach, we construct the Reasoned Safety Alignment (ReSA) dataset, comprising 80K samples that teach models to reason through direct responses and then analyze their safety. Experimental results demonstrate that our approach achieves the Pareto frontier with superior safety capability while decreasing over-refusal rates. Notably, the fine-tuned model maintains general reasoning capabilities on benchmarks like MMLU, MATH500, and HumanEval. Besides, our method equips models with the ability to perform safe completion, while post-hoc detection methods can only directly reject sensitive, harmful queries (e.g., self-harm). Our results show that inference-time strategies alone are insufficient, highlighting the necessity of safety training, and we find even $500$ samples can yield performance comparable to the entire dataset, suggesting a promising path for data-efficient safety alignment. The dataset is publicly available at: https://huggingface.co/datasets/ByteDance-Seed/ReSA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11629","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:07:09Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:07:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-09T02:08:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11686","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11686","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11686","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofei","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Xiaofei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangqing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shangqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Do Code Semantics Help? A Comprehensive Study on Execution Trace-Based Information for Code Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T08:38:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:09:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-18T11:44:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:36:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-24T07:06:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-25T00:29:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Code Large Language Models (Code LLMs) have opened a new era in programming with their impressive capabilities. However, recent research has revealed critical limitations in their ability to reason about runtime behavior and understand the actual functionality of programs, which poses significant challenges for their post-training and practical deployment. Specifically, Code LLMs encounter two principal issues: (1) a lack of proficiency in reasoning about program execution behavior, as they struggle to interpret what programs actually do during runtime, and (2) the inconsistent and fragmented representation of semantic information, such as execution traces, across existing methods, which hinders their ability to generalize and reason effectively. These challenges underscore the necessity for more systematic approaches to enhance the reasoning capabilities of Code LLMs. To address these issues, we introduce a generic framework to support integrating semantic information~(e.g., execution trace) to code task-relevant prompts, and conduct a comprehensive study to explore the role of semantic information in enhancing the reasoning ability of Code LLMs accordingly. Specifically, we focus on investigating the usefulness of trace-based semantic information in boosting supervised fine-tuning~(SFT) and post-phase inference of Code LLMs. The experimental results surprisingly disagree with previous works and demonstrate that semantic information has limited usefulness for SFT and test time scaling of Code LLM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP2025-findings https://openreview.net/forum?id=d4ICISW2T4"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11686","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:08:37Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:08:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-25T02:25:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11697","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11697","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11697","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wan-Lei","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Wan-Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shihai","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Shihai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajie","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Jiajie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuecang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xuecang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards the Distributed Large-scale k-NN Graph Construction by Graph Merge"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T08:56:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:10:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-21T14:59:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:57:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T02:31:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:23:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-19T09:19:15Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v4; the paper needs considerable revision"},{"date":"2026-01-21T02:15:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In order to support the real-time interaction with LLMs and the instant search or the instant recommendation on social media, it becomes an imminent problem to build a k-NN graph or an indexing graph for the massive number of vectorized multimedia data. In such scenarios, the scale of the data or the scale of the graph may exceed the processing capacity of a single machine. This paper aims to address the graph construction problem of such scale via efficient graph merge. For the graph construction on a single node, two generic and highly parallelizable algorithms, namely Two-way Merge and Multi-way Merge are proposed to merge subgraphs into one. For the graph construction across multiple nodes, a multi-node procedure based on Two-way Merge is presented. The procedure makes it feasible to construct a large-scale k-NN graph/indexing graph on either a single node or multiple nodes when the data size exceeds the memory capacity of one node. Extensive experiments are conducted on both large-scale k-NN graph and indexing graph construction. For the k-NN graph construction, the large-scale and high-quality k-NN graphs are constructed by graph merge in parallel. Typically, a billion-scale k-NN graph can be built in approximately 17h when only three nodes are employed. For the indexing graph construction, similar NN search performance as the original indexing graph is achieved with the merged indexing graphs while requiring much less time of construction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"the paper needs considerable revision"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11697","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:08:54Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:08:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:04:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11724","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11724","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11724","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wa-Kin","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Wa-Kin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun-Cheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jun-Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shang-Tse","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shang-Tse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DRAG: Data Reconstruction Attack using Guided Diffusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T09:26:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:12:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rise of large foundation models, split inference (SI) has emerged as a popular computational paradigm for deploying models across lightweight edge devices and cloud servers, addressing data privacy and computational cost concerns. However, most existing data reconstruction attacks have focused on smaller CNN classification models, leaving the privacy risks of foundation models in SI settings largely unexplored. To address this gap, we propose a novel data reconstruction attack based on guided diffusion, which leverages the rich prior knowledge embedded in a latent diffusion model (LDM) pre-trained on a large-scale dataset. Our method performs iterative reconstruction on the LDM's learned image prior, effectively generating high-fidelity images resembling the original data from their intermediate representations (IR). Extensive experiments demonstrate that our approach significantly outperforms state-of-the-art methods, both qualitatively and quantitatively, in reconstructing data from deep-layer IRs of the vision foundation model. The results highlight the urgent need for more robust privacy protection mechanisms for large models in SI scenarios. Code is available at: https://github.com/ntuaislab/DRAG."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11724","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:09:35Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:09:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:09:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11803","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11803","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11803","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eden","familyName":"Mama","name":"Mama, Eden","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liel","familyName":"Sheri","name":"Sheri, Liel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yehudit","familyName":"Aperstein","name":"Aperstein, Yehudit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Apartsin","name":"Apartsin, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Fuzzy Speech to Medical Insight: Benchmarking LLMs on Noisy Patient Narratives"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T11:34:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:17:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The widespread adoption of large language models (LLMs) in healthcare raises critical questions about their ability to interpret patient-generated narratives, which are often informal, ambiguous, and noisy. Existing benchmarks typically rely on clean, structured clinical text, offering limited insight into model performance under realistic conditions. In this work, we present a novel synthetic dataset designed to simulate patient self-descriptions characterized by varying levels of linguistic noise, fuzzy language, and layperson terminology. Our dataset comprises clinically consistent scenarios annotated with ground-truth diagnoses, spanning a spectrum of communication clarity to reflect diverse real-world reporting styles. Using this benchmark, we fine-tune and evaluate several state-of-the-art models (LLMs), including BERT-based and encoder-decoder T5 models. To support reproducibility and future research, we release the Noisy Diagnostic Benchmark (NDB), a structured dataset of noisy, synthetic patient descriptions designed to stress-test and compare the diagnostic capabilities of large language models (LLMs) under realistic linguistic conditions. We made the benchmark available for the community: https://github.com/lielsheri/PatientSignal"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11803","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:11:33Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:11:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:11:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11815","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11815","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11815","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiduo","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Haiduo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuwei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Fuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhua","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhenhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanwu","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Xuanwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengju","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Pengju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emad","familyName":"Barsoum","name":"Barsoum, Emad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SpecVLM: Fast Speculative Decoding in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T11:53:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:18:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-21T03:35:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:45:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speculative decoding is a powerful way to accelerate autoregressive large language models (LLMs), but directly porting it to vision-language models (VLMs) faces unique systems constraints: the prefill stage is dominated by visual tokens whose count scales with image resolution and video length, inflating both compute and memory, especially the key-value (KV) cache. We study speculative decoding for VLMs and introduce SpecVLM, a practical system that (1) establishes a strong EAGLE-2-style baseline, EagleVLM, delivering 1.5--2.3x end-to-end speedups over full autoregressive inference, and (2) further accelerates VLM inference with an elastic visual compressor that adaptively selects among pruning, pooling, convolution, and resampler primitives to balance FLOPs/parameters and accuracy per input. To avoid costly offline distillation corpora, we propose an online-logit distillation protocol that trains the draft model with on-the-fly teacher logits and penultimate features using a combined cross-entropy and Smooth L1 objective, eliminating storage and preprocessing while remaining compute-efficient. This protocol reveals a training-time scaling effect: longer online training monotonically increases the draft model's average accepted length, improving speculative efficiency. Empirically, SpecVLM achieves additional acceleration, culminating in 2.5--2.9x end-to-end speedups within 5 epochs across LLaVA and MMMU, consistently over resolutions and task difficulties, while preserving the target model's output distribution (lossless decoding). Our code is available at https://github.com/haiduo/SpecVLM."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11815","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:11:51Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:11:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:56:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11816","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11816","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11816","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Filip","familyName":"Sondej","name":"Sondej, Filip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yushi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Collapse of Irrelevant Representations (CIR) Ensures Robust and Non-Disruptive LLM Unlearning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T11:55:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:18:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-13T17:23:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-14T01:55:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current unlearning and safety training methods consistently fail to remove dangerous knowledge from language models. We identify the root cause - unlearning targets representations which are too general - and develop a highly selective technique that unlearns robustly while preserving general performance.\n Our method performs PCA on activations and module-output gradients to identify subspaces containing common representations, then collapses these subspaces before computing unlearning updates, a technique we term Collapse of Irrelevant Representations (CIR). This avoids unlearning general knowledge and targets only representations specific to the facts being unlearned.\n When unlearning bio- and cyber-hazardous facts from Llama-3.1-8B, we achieve over 30x greater reduction in post-attack accuracy than the best baseline (Circuit Breakers), while disrupting general performance 30x less, and using less than 3 GPU-seconds per fact.\n Thus, by disentangling harmful and benign capabilities at the level of representations, CIR enables robust and non-disruptive unlearning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11816","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:11:52Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:11:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-14T03:10:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11817","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11817","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11817","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liying","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Liying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoli","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaoli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuanmin","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Chuanmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siwei","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Siwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MAFS: Masked Autoencoder for Infrared-Visible Image Fusion and Semantic Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T11:55:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:18:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Infrared-visible image fusion methods aim at generating fused images with good visual quality and also facilitate the performance of high-level tasks. Indeed, existing semantic-driven methods have considered semantic information injection for downstream applications. However, none of them investigates the potential for reciprocal promotion between pixel-wise image fusion and cross-modal feature fusion perception tasks from a macroscopic task-level perspective. To address this limitation, we propose a unified network for image fusion and semantic segmentation. MAFS is a parallel structure, containing a fusion sub-network and a segmentation sub-network. On the one hand, We devise a heterogeneous feature fusion strategy to enhance semantic-aware capabilities for image fusion. On the other hand, by cascading the fusion sub-network and a segmentation backbone, segmentation-related knowledge is transferred to promote feature-level fusion-based segmentation. Within the framework, we design a novel multi-stage Transformer decoder to aggregate fine-grained multi-scale fused features efficiently. Additionally, a dynamic factor based on the max-min fairness allocation principle is introduced to generate adaptive weights of two tasks and guarantee smooth training in a multi-task manner. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our approach achieves competitive results compared with state-of-the-art methods. The code is available at https://github.com/Abraham-Einstein/MAFS/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by TIP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11817","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:11:54Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:11:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:11:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11840","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11840","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11840","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tim","familyName":"Lebailly","name":"Lebailly, Tim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vijay","familyName":"Veerabadran","name":"Veerabadran, Vijay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satwik","familyName":"Kottur","name":"Kottur, Satwik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karl","familyName":"Ridgeway","name":"Ridgeway, Karl","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael Louis","familyName":"Iuzzolino","name":"Iuzzolino, Michael Louis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Synthetic Captions for Open-Vocabulary Zero-Shot Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T12:26:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:20:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative vision-language models (VLMs) exhibit strong high-level image understanding but lack spatially dense alignment between vision and language modalities, as our findings indicate. Orthogonal to advancements in generative VLMs, another line of research has focused on representation learning for vision-language alignment, targeting zero-shot inference for dense tasks like segmentation. In this work, we bridge these two directions by densely aligning images with synthetic descriptions generated by VLMs. Synthetic captions are inexpensive, scalable, and easy to generate, making them an excellent source of high-level semantic understanding for dense alignment methods. Empirically, our approach outperforms prior work on standard zero-shot open-vocabulary segmentation benchmarks/datasets, while also being more data-efficient."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICCV 2025 CDEL Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11840","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:12:28Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:12:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:12:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11864","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11864","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11864","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lichao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Lichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sasha","familyName":"Behrouzi","name":"Behrouzi, Sasha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamadreza","familyName":"Rostami","name":"Rostami, Mohamadreza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maximilian","familyName":"Thang","name":"Thang, Maximilian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stjepan","familyName":"Picek","name":"Picek, Stjepan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmad-Reza","familyName":"Sadeghi","name":"Sadeghi, Ahmad-Reza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NeuroStrike: Neuron-Level Attacks on Aligned LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T12:38:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:20:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-17T13:02:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T02:47:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-26T08:18:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-27T00:37:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.14722/ndss.2026.230660","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Safety alignment is critical for the ethical deployment of large language models (LLMs), guiding them to avoid generating harmful or unethical content. Current alignment techniques, such as supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning from human feedback, remain fragile and can be bypassed by carefully crafted adversarial prompts. Unfortunately, such attacks rely on trial and error, lack generalizability across models, and are constrained by scalability and reliability.\n This paper presents NeuroStrike, a novel and generalizable attack framework that exploits a fundamental vulnerability introduced by alignment techniques: the reliance on sparse, specialized safety neurons responsible for detecting and suppressing harmful inputs. We apply NeuroStrike to both white-box and black-box settings: In the white-box setting, NeuroStrike identifies safety neurons through feedforward activation analysis and prunes them during inference to disable safety mechanisms. In the black-box setting, we propose the first LLM profiling attack, which leverages safety neuron transferability by training adversarial prompt generators on open-weight surrogate models and then deploying them against black-box and proprietary targets. We evaluate NeuroStrike on over 20 open-weight LLMs from major LLM developers. By removing less than 0.6% of neurons in targeted layers, NeuroStrike achieves an average attack success rate (ASR) of 76.9% using only vanilla malicious prompts. Moreover, Neurostrike generalizes to four multimodal LLMs with 100% ASR on unsafe image inputs. Safety neurons transfer effectively across architectures, raising ASR to 78.5% on 11 fine-tuned models and 77.7% on five distilled models. The black-box LLM profiling attack achieves an average ASR of 63.7% across five black-box models, including the Google Gemini family."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11864","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-16T03:13:02Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:13:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-27T02:21:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11865","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11865","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11865","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Travis","familyName":"Davies","name":"Davies, Travis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunxin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yunxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huxian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Huxian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luhui","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Luhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tenma: Robust Cross-Embodiment Robot Manipulation with Diffusion Transformer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T12:39:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:20:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Scaling Transformer policies and diffusion models has advanced robotic manipulation, yet combining these techniques in lightweight, cross-embodiment learning settings remains challenging. We study design choices that most affect stability and performance for diffusion-transformer policies trained on heterogeneous, multimodal robot data, and introduce Tenma, a lightweight diffusion-transformer for bi-manual arm control. Tenma integrates multiview RGB, proprioception, and language via a cross-embodiment normalizer that maps disparate state/action spaces into a shared latent space; a Joint State-Time encoder for temporally aligned observation learning with inference speed boosts; and a diffusion action decoder optimized for training stability and learning capacity. Across benchmarks and under matched compute, Tenma achieves an average success rate of 88.95% in-distribution and maintains strong performance under object and scene shifts, substantially exceeding baseline policies whose best in-distribution average is 18.12%. Despite using moderate data scale, Tenma delivers robust manipulation and generalization, indicating the great potential for multimodal and cross-embodiment learning strategies for further augmenting the capacity of transformer-based imitation learning policies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11865","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:13:03Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:13:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:13:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11915","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11915","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11915","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aadil Gani","familyName":"Ganie","name":"Ganie, Aadil Gani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Uncertainty in Authorship: Why Perfect AI Detection Is Mathematically Impossible"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T13:33:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:23:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) become more advanced, it is increasingly difficult to distinguish between human-written and AI-generated text. This paper draws a conceptual parallel between quantum uncertainty and the limits of authorship detection in natural language. We argue that there is a fundamental trade-off: the more confidently one tries to identify whether a text was written by a human or an AI, the more one risks disrupting the text's natural flow and authenticity. This mirrors the tension between precision and disturbance found in quantum systems. We explore how current detection methods--such as stylometry, watermarking, and neural classifiers--face inherent limitations. Enhancing detection accuracy often leads to changes in the AI's output, making other features less reliable. In effect, the very act of trying to detect AI authorship introduces uncertainty elsewhere in the text. Our analysis shows that when AI-generated text closely mimics human writing, perfect detection becomes not just technologically difficult but theoretically impossible. We address counterarguments and discuss the broader implications for authorship, ethics, and policy. Ultimately, we suggest that the challenge of AI-text detection is not just a matter of better tools--it reflects a deeper, unavoidable tension in the nature of language itself."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11915","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:14:14Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:14:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:14:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11921","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11921","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11921","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Helene","familyName":"Tenzer","name":"Tenzer, Helene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oumnia","familyName":"Abidi","name":"Abidi, Oumnia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Feuerriegel","name":"Feuerriegel, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Designing LLMs for cultural sensitivity: Evidence from English-Japanese translation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T13:37:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:24:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-17T08:40:48Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v2; Posted premature without permission of all authors"},{"date":"2025-12-18T01:29:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used in everyday communication, including multilingual interactions across different cultural contexts. While LLMs can now generate near-perfect literal translations, it remains unclear whether LLMs support culturally appropriate communication. In this paper, we analyze the cultural sensitivity of different LLM designs when applied to English-Japanese translations of workplace e-mails. Here, we vary the prompting strategies: (1) naive \"just translate\" prompts, (2) audience-targeted prompts specifying the recipient's cultural background, and (3) instructional prompts with explicit guidance on Japanese communication norms. Using a mixed-methods study, we then analyze culture-specific language patterns to evaluate how well translations adapt to cultural norms. Further, we examine the appropriateness of the tone of the translations as perceived by native speakers. We find that culturally-tailored prompting can improve cultural fit, based on which we offer recommendations for designing culturally inclusive LLMs in multilingual settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Posted premature without permission of all authors"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11921","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:14:25Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:14:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-18T03:06:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11937","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11937","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11937","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Sallinen","name":"Sallinen, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Krsteski","name":"Krsteski, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Teiletche","name":"Teiletche, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marc-Antoine","familyName":"Allard","name":"Allard, Marc-Antoine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baptiste","familyName":"Lecoeur","name":"Lecoeur, Baptiste","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabrice","familyName":"Nemo","name":"Nemo, Fabrice","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Kalajdzic","name":"Kalajdzic, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Meyer","name":"Meyer, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mary-Anne","familyName":"Hartley","name":"Hartley, Mary-Anne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MMORE: Massive Multimodal Open RAG \u0026amp; Extraction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"D.2.0; E.m","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T13:56:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:24:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce MMORE, an open-source pipeline for Massive Multimodal Open RetrievalAugmented Generation and Extraction, designed to ingest, transform, and retrieve knowledge from heterogeneous document formats at scale. MMORE supports more than fifteen file types, including text, tables, images, emails, audio, and video, and processes them into a unified format to enable downstream applications for LLMs. The architecture offers modular, distributed processing, enabling scalable parallelization across CPUs and GPUs. On processing benchmarks, MMORE demonstrates a 3.8-fold speedup over single-node baselines and 40% higher accuracy than Docling on scanned PDFs. The pipeline integrates hybrid dense-sparse retrieval and supports both interactive APIs and batch RAG endpoints. Evaluated on PubMedQA, MMORE-augmented medical LLMs improve biomedical QA accuracy with increasing retrieval depth. MMORE provides a robust, extensible foundation for deploying task-agnostic RAG systems on diverse, real-world multimodal data. The codebase is available at https://github.com/swiss-ai/mmore."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper was originally submitted to the CODEML workshop for ICML 2025. 9 pages (including references and appendices)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11937","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:14:54Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:14:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:14:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11943","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11943","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11943","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonin","familyName":"Sulc","name":"Sulc, Antonin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thorsten","familyName":"Hellert","name":"Hellert, Thorsten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agentic System with Modal Logic for Autonomous Diagnostics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:03:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:25:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T16:39:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T01:08:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-18T00:10:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:14:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The development of intelligent agents, particularly those powered by language models (LMs), has shown a critical role in various environments that require intelligent and autonomous decision-making. Environments are not passive testing grounds, and they represent the data required for agents to learn and exhibit in very challenging conditions that require adaptive, complex, and autonomous capacity to make decisions. While the paradigm of scaling models and datasets has led to remarkable emergent capabilities, we argue that scaling the structure, fidelity, and logical consistency of agent reasoning within these environments is a crucial, yet underexplored, dimension of AI research. This paper introduces a neuro-symbolic multi-agent architecture where the belief states of individual agents are formally represented as Kripke models. This foundational choice enables them to reason about known concepts of \\emph{possibility} and \\emph{necessity} using the formal language of modal logic. In this work, we use immutable, domain-specific knowledge to make an informed root cause diagnosis, which is encoded as logical constraints essential for proper, reliable, and explainable diagnosis. In the proposed model, we show constraints that actively guide the hypothesis generation of LMs, effectively preventing them from reaching physically or logically untenable conclusions. In a high-fidelity simulated particle accelerator environment, our system successfully diagnoses complex, cascading failures by combining the powerful semantic intuition of LMs with the rigorous, verifiable validation of modal logic and a factual world model and showcasing a viable path toward more robust, reliable, and verifiable autonomous agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11943","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:15:04Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:15:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:40:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11961","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11961","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11961","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxiao","familyName":"Huo","name":"Huo, Mingxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hewei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinfeng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jinfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zheyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huilin","familyName":"Tai","name":"Tai, Huilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spec-LLaVA: Accelerating Vision-Language Models with Dynamic Tree-Based Speculative Decoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:16:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:26:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) enable powerful multimodal reasoning but suffer from slow autoregressive inference, limiting their deployment in real-time applications. We introduce Spec-LLaVA, a system that applies speculative decoding to accelerate VLMs without sacrificing output quality. Spec-LLaVA pairs a lightweight draft VLM with a large target model: the draft speculates future tokens, which the target verifies in parallel, allowing multiple tokens to be generated per step. To maximize efficiency, we design a dynamic tree-based verification algorithm that adaptively expands and prunes speculative branches using draft model confidence. On MS COCO out-of-domain images, Spec-LLaVA achieves up to 3.28$\\times$ faster decoding on LLaVA-1.5 (7B, 13B) with no loss in generation quality. This work presents a lossless acceleration framework for VLMs using dynamic tree-structured speculative decoding, opening a path toward practical real-time multimodal assistants. Importantly, the lightweight draft model design makes the framework amenable to resource-constrained or on-device deployment settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7pages, accepted by ICML TTODLer-FM workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11961","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:15:31Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:15:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:15:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12040","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12040","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12040","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haocheng","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Haocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Da","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Da","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyu","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Junyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuelong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xuelong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring Efficient Open-Vocabulary Segmentation in the Remote Sensing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T15:24:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:31:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-15T14:41:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T01:37:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T06:06:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-19T00:32:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Open-Vocabulary Remote Sensing Image Segmentation (OVRSIS), an emerging task that adapts Open-Vocabulary Segmentation (OVS) to the remote sensing (RS) domain, remains underexplored due to the absence of a unified evaluation benchmark and the domain gap between natural and RS images. To bridge these gaps, we first establish a standardized OVRSIS benchmark (\\textbf{OVRSISBench}) based on widely-used RS segmentation datasets, enabling consistent evaluation across methods. Using this benchmark, we comprehensively evaluate several representative OVS/OVRSIS models and reveal their limitations when directly applied to remote sensing scenarios. Building on these insights, we propose \\textbf{RSKT-Seg}, a novel open-vocabulary segmentation framework tailored for remote sensing. RSKT-Seg integrates three key components: (1) a Multi-Directional Cost Map Aggregation (RS-CMA) module that captures rotation-invariant visual cues by computing vision-language cosine similarities across multiple directions; (2) an Efficient Cost Map Fusion (RS-Fusion) transformer, which jointly models spatial and semantic dependencies with a lightweight dimensionality reduction strategy; and (3) a Remote Sensing Knowledge Transfer (RS-Transfer) module that injects pre-trained knowledge and facilitates domain adaptation via enhanced upsampling. Extensive experiments on the benchmark show that RSKT-Seg consistently outperforms strong OVS baselines by +3.8 mIoU and +5.9 mACC, while achieving 2x faster inference through efficient aggregation. Our code is \\href{https://github.com/LiBingyu01/RSKT-Seg}{\\textcolor{blue}{here}}."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12040","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:17:30Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:17:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-19T02:23:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12053","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12053","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12053","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujun","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhekai","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhekai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Song","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Song","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LEGO: Spatial Accelerator Generation and Optimization for Tensor Applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T15:36:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:32:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/hpca61900.2025.00101","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern tensor applications, especially foundation models and generative AI applications require multiple input modalities (both vision and language), which increases the demand for flexible accelerator architecture. Existing frameworks suffer from the trade-off between design flexibility and productivity of RTL generation: either limited to very few hand-written templates or cannot automatically generate the RTL. To address this challenge, we propose the LEGO framework, which targets tensor applications and automatically generates spatial architecture design and outputs synthesizable RTL code without handwritten RTL design templates. Leveraging the affine-transformation-based architecture representation, LEGO front end finds interconnections between function units, synthesizes the memory system, and fuses different spatial dataflow designs based on data reuse analysis. LEGO back end then translates the hardware in a primitive-level graph to perform lower-level optimizations, and applies a set of linear-programming algorithms to optimally insert pipeline registers and reduce the overhead of unused logic when switching spatial dataflows. Our evaluation demonstrates that LEGO can achieve 3.2x speedup and 2.4x energy efficiency compared to previous work Gemmini, and can generate one architecture for diverse modern foundation models in generative AI applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The first two authors have equal contributions; Published as a conference paper in HPCA 2025; 13 pages, 14 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12053","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-16T03:17:50Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:17:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:17:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12104","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12104","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12104","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongyue","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Zongyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyuan","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Siyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaochun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shaochun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiran","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shenran","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shenran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxin","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Yuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haitao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingyao","familyName":"Ai","name":"Ai, Qingyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yiqun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weixing","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Weixing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"JustEva: A Toolkit to Evaluate LLM Fairness in Legal Knowledge Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T16:31:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:36:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) into legal practice raises pressing concerns about judicial fairness, particularly due to the nature of their \"black-box\" processes. This study introduces JustEva, a comprehensive, open-source evaluation toolkit designed to measure LLM fairness in legal tasks. JustEva features several advantages: (1) a structured label system covering 65 extra-legal factors; (2) three core fairness metrics - inconsistency, bias, and imbalanced inaccuracy; (3) robust statistical inference methods; and (4) informative visualizations. The toolkit supports two types of experiments, enabling a complete evaluation workflow: (1) generating structured outputs from LLMs using a provided dataset, and (2) conducting statistical analysis and inference on LLMs' outputs through regression and other statistical methods. Empirical application of JustEva reveals significant fairness deficiencies in current LLMs, highlighting the lack of fair and trustworthy LLM legal tools. JustEva offers a convenient tool and methodological foundation for evaluating and improving algorithmic fairness in the legal domain."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper has been accepted at CIKM 2025 (Demo Track)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12104","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:19:05Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:19:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:19:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12130","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12130","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12130","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ariana","familyName":"Sahitaj","name":"Sahitaj, Ariana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiaao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pia Wenzel","familyName":"Neves","name":"Neves, Pia Wenzel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fedor","familyName":"Splitt","name":"Splitt, Fedor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Premtim","familyName":"Sahitaj","name":"Sahitaj, Premtim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charlott","familyName":"Jakob","name":"Jakob, Charlott","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Veronika","familyName":"Solopova","name":"Solopova, Veronika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vera","familyName":"Schmitt","name":"Schmitt, Vera","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"XplaiNLP at CheckThat! 2025: Multilingual Subjectivity Detection with Finetuned Transformers and Prompt-Based Inference with Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T16:53:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:37:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This notebook reports the XplaiNLP submission to the CheckThat! 2025 shared task on multilingual subjectivity detection. We evaluate two approaches: (1) supervised fine-tuning of transformer encoders, EuroBERT, XLM-RoBERTa, and German-BERT, on monolingual and machine-translated training data; and (2) zero-shot prompting using two LLMs: o3-mini for Annotation (rule-based labelling) and gpt-4.1-mini for DoubleDown (contrastive rewriting) and Perspective (comparative reasoning). The Annotation Approach achieves 1st place in the Italian monolingual subtask with an F_1 score of 0.8104, outperforming the baseline of 0.6941. In the Romanian zero-shot setting, the fine-tuned XLM-RoBERTa model obtains an F_1 score of 0.7917, ranking 3rd and exceeding the baseline of 0.6461. The same model also performs reliably in the multilingual task and improves over the baseline in Greek. For German, a German-BERT model fine-tuned on translated training data from typologically related languages yields competitive performance over the baseline. In contrast, performance in the Ukrainian and Polish zero-shot settings falls slightly below the respective baselines, reflecting the challenge of generalization in low-resource cross-lingual scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12130","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:19:41Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:19:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:19:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12136","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12136","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12136","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomer","familyName":"Bitan","name":"Bitan, Tomer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tal","familyName":"Kadosh","name":"Kadosh, Tal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erel","familyName":"Kaplan","name":"Kaplan, Erel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shira","familyName":"Meiri","name":"Meiri, Shira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Le","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Morales","name":"Morales, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niranjan","familyName":"Hasabnis","name":"Hasabnis, Niranjan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gal","familyName":"Oren","name":"Oren, Gal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UniPar: A Unified LLM-Based Framework for Parallel and Accelerated Code Translation in HPC"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T17:03:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:38:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Translating programs between various parallel programming languages is an important problem in the high-performance computing (HPC) community. Existing tools for this problem are either too narrow in scope and/or outdated. Recent explosive growth in the popularity of large language models (LLMs) and their ability to generate and translate code offers a potential alternative approach. Toward that end, we first need to systematically evaluate the ability of LLMs to translate between parallel languages.\n In this work, we introduce UniPar, a systematic evaluation framework for LLM-based parallel code translation. Specifically, in this work, we target translations between serial code, CUDA, and OpenMP. Our goal is to assess how well current instruction-tuned LLMs -- specifically GPT-4o-mini and LLaMA-3.3-70B-Instruct -- can be used out of the box or enhanced through known strategies. We evaluated four major usage modes: hyperparameter optimization for decoding, zero- and few-shot prompting, supervised fine-tuning, and iterative feedback through compiler-based repair. As a part of the evaluation, we construct a new dataset called PARATRANS, covering both serial-to-parallel translation and cross-paradigm transformations.\n Our findings reveal that while off-the-shelf models struggle under the default settings (e.g., GPT-4o-mini achieves only 46% compilation and 15% functional correctness), our UniPar methodology -- combining fine-tuning, hyperparameter tuning, and compiler-guided repair -- improves performance by up to 2X (69% compilation and 33% correctness). We believe that our findings will provide useful insights for researchers to further improve LLMs for the parallel language translation problem.\n UniPar source code and PARATRANS dataset are available at our GitHub repository https://github.com/Scientific-Computing-Lab/UniPar_AI."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to IEEE HPEC conference 2025. 9 pages, incl references"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12136","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:19:49Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:19:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:19:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12141","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12141","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12141","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihao","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Weihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Long","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Kang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Mei","name":"Mei, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiaheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When MoE Meets Blockchain: A Trustworthy Distributed Framework of Large Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T17:05:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:38:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:51:29Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v2; We need to revise the content of this article"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:15:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As an enabling architecture of Large Models (LMs), Mixture of Experts (MoE) has become prevalent thanks to its sparsely-gated mechanism, which lowers computational overhead while maintaining learning performance comparable to dense LMs. The essence of MoE lies in utilizing a group of neural networks (called experts) with each specializing in different types of tasks, along with a trainable gating network that selectively activates a subset of these experts to handle specific tasks. Traditional cloud-based MoE encounters challenges such as prolonged response latency, high bandwidth consumption, and data privacy leakage. To address these issues, researchers have proposed to deploy MoE over distributed edge networks. However, a key concern of distributed MoE frameworks is the lack of trust in data interactions among distributed experts without the surveillance of any trusted authority, and thereby prone to potential attacks such as data manipulation. In response to the security issues of traditional distributed MoE, we propose a blockchain-aided trustworthy MoE (B-MoE) framework that consists of three layers: the edge layer, the blockchain layer, and the storage layer. In this framework, the edge layer employs the activated experts downloaded from the storage layer to process the learning tasks, while the blockchain layer functions as a decentralized trustworthy network to trace, verify, and record the computational results of the experts from the edge layer. The experimental results demonstrate that B-MoE is more robust to data manipulation attacks than traditional distributed MoE during both the training and inference processes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"We need to revise the content of this article"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12141","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:19:55Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:19:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:13:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12152","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12152","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12152","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Synthia","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Synthia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sai Teja","familyName":"Peddinti","name":"Peddinti, Sai Teja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nina","familyName":"Taft","name":"Taft, Nina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nick","familyName":"Feamster","name":"Feamster, Nick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond PII: How Users Attempt to Estimate and Mitigate Implicit LLM Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T17:17:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:39:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT can infer personal attributes from seemingly innocuous text, raising privacy risks beyond memorized data leakage. While prior work has demonstrated these risks, little is known about how users estimate and respond. We conducted a survey with 240 U.S. participants who judged text snippets for inference risks, reported concern levels, and attempted rewrites to block inference. We compared their rewrites with those generated by ChatGPT and Rescriber, a state-of-the-art sanitization tool. Results show that participants struggled to anticipate inference, performing a little better than chance. User rewrites were effective in just 28\\% of cases - better than Rescriber but worse than ChatGPT. We examined our participants' rewriting strategies, and observed that while paraphrasing was the most common strategy it is also the least effective; instead abstraction and adding ambiguity were more successful. Our work highlights the importance of inference-aware design in LLM interactions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12152","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:20:11Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:20:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:20:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12159","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12159","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12159","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyu","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Jingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongyi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yintong","familyName":"Huo","name":"Huo, Yintong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael R.","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Michael R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EfficientUICoder: A Bidirectional Token Compression Framework for Efficient MLLM-Based UI Code Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T17:23:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:39:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-27T08:23:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-30T00:28:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3808114","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Language Models have demonstrated exceptional performance in UI2Code tasks, significantly enhancing website development efficiency. However, these tasks incur substantially higher computational overhead than traditional code generation due to the large number of input image tokens and extensive output code tokens required. Our comprehensive study identifies significant redundancies in both image and code tokens that exacerbate computational complexity and hinder focus on key UI elements, resulting in excessively lengthy and often invalid HTML files. We propose EfficientUICoder, a compression framework for efficient UI code generation with three key components. First, Element and Layout-aware Token Compression preserves essential UI information by detecting element regions and constructing UI element trees. Second, Region-aware Token Refinement leverages attention scores to discard low-attention tokens from selected regions while integrating high-attention tokens from unselected regions. Third, Adaptive Duplicate Token Suppression dynamically reduces repetitive generation by tracking HTML/CSS structure frequencies and applying exponential penalties. Extensive experiments show EfficientUICoder achieves a 55%-60% compression ratio without compromising webpage quality and delivers superior efficiency improvements: reducing computational cost by 44.9%, generated tokens by 41.4%, prefill time by 46.6%, and inference time by 48.8% on 34B-level MLLMs. Code is available at https://github.com/WebPAI/EfficientUICoder."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in the Proceedings of the 2026 ACM International Conference on the Foundations of Software Engineering (FSE 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12159","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-16T03:20:21Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:20:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-30T04:06:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12168","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12168","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12168","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timothy","familyName":"Rupprecht","name":"Rupprecht, Timothy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enfu","familyName":"Nan","name":"Nan, Enfu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arash","familyName":"Akbari","name":"Akbari, Arash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arman","familyName":"Akbari","name":"Akbari, Arman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Priyanka","familyName":"Maan","name":"Maan, Priyanka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sean","familyName":"Duffy","name":"Duffy, Sean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Pu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yumei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yumei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Kaeli","name":"Kaeli, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanzhi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yanzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RAGs to Riches: RAG-like Few-shot Learning for Large Language Model Role-playing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T17:31:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:40:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Role-playing Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in high-stakes domains such as healthcare, education, and governance, where failures can directly impact user trust and well-being. A cost effective paradigm for LLM role-playing is few-shot learning, but existing approaches often cause models to break character in unexpected and potentially harmful ways, especially when interacting with hostile users. Inspired by Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), we reformulate LLM role-playing into a text retrieval problem and propose a new prompting framework called RAGs-to-Riches, which leverages curated reference demonstrations to condition LLM responses. We evaluate our framework with LLM-as-a-judge preference voting and introduce two novel token-level ROUGE metrics: Intersection over Output (IOO) to quantity how much an LLM improvises and Intersection over References (IOR) to measure few-shot demonstrations utilization rate during the evaluation tasks. When simulating interactions with a hostile user, our prompting strategy incorporates in its responses during inference an average of 35% more tokens from the reference demonstrations. As a result, across 453 role-playing interactions, our models are consistently judged as being more authentic, and remain in-character more often than zero-shot and in-context Learning (ICL) methods. Our method presents a scalable strategy for building robust, human-aligned LLM role-playing frameworks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12168","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:20:33Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:20:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:20:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":73,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?page%5Bsize%5D=1000\u0026sort=name\u0026client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-15T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-16T16%3A00%3A00Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22+OR+MoE+OR+MoEs%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22+OR+MoE+OR+MoEs%29%29+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28inference+OR+serving+OR+parallel+OR+parallelism+OR+communication+OR+communications+OR+kernel+OR+kernels+OR+compiler+OR+compilers+OR+GPU+OR+GPUs+OR+accelerator+OR+accelerators%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28inference+OR+serving+OR+parallel+OR+parallelism+OR+communication+OR+communications+OR+kernel+OR+kernels+OR+compiler+OR+compilers+OR+GPU+OR+GPUs+OR+accelerator+OR+accelerators%29%29"}}